OAI_PAPER({"slug": "An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026", "title": "An asymptotic formula for the number of totients", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026.pdf", "family": "024", "family_title": "An asymptotic formula for the number of totients", "abstract_html": "Let \\(V(x)\\) count the distinct values of Euler's totient function up to <i>x</i>. We give an explicit asymptotic equivalent for \\(V(x)\\). Its coefficient is a uniform limit of functions defined from finite arithmetic data. We also prove that \\(V(cx)/V(x)\\to c\\) as \\(x\\to\\infty\\) for every fixed <i>c</i> &amp;gt; 0, answering a question of Erdős and Hall.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 2.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal V=\\{\\varphi(n):n\\ge1\\},\\qquad\n  V(x)=\\#\\{v\\in\\mathcal V:v\\le x\\},\\qquad\n  \\ell(v)=\\min\\{n\\ge1:\\varphi(n)=v\\}.\n  \\]\n  All logarithms are natural, and \\(\\log_j\\) denotes the \\(j\\)-fold iterated logarithm.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j\\ge1\\), put\n  \\[\n  a_j=(j+1)\\log(j+1)-j\\log j-1\n      =\\int_j^{j+1}\\log t\\,dt. \\tag{2.1}\n  \\]\n  Let \\(\\rho\\in(0,1)\\) be the unique root of\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j\\ge1}a_j\\rho^j=1.\n  \\]\n  Define\n  \\[\n  \\lambda=\\log(1/\\rho),\\qquad\n  \\gamma=\\left(\\sum_{j\\ge1}ja_j\\rho^j\\right)^{-1},\n  \\qquad\n  g_0=1,\\qquad\n  g_j=\\sum_{d=1}^ja_dg_{j-d}. \\tag{2.2}\n  \\]\n  Thus\n  \\[\n  C_0=\\frac1{2\\lambda},\\qquad\n  D_0=\\frac{1+\\log(\\lambda/\\gamma)}{\\lambda}-\\frac12\n  \\]\n  are the constants in the classical bounded-factor estimate for \\(V(x)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For sufficiently large \\(x\\), put\n  \\[\n  B=\\log_2x,\\qquad\n  m=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\log B-\\log_2B}{\\lambda}\\right\\rfloor,\n  \\qquad\n  \\theta=\\frac{\\log B-\\log_2B}{\\lambda}-m, \\tag{2.3}\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  G_j=\\frac{B^j}{j!\\prod_{i=1}^jg_i}\\quad(0\\le j\\le m),\n  \\qquad G_0=1. \\tag{2.4}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The following arithmetic construction defines the coefficient independently of \\(V(x)\\). Fix \\(s\\in[0,1)\\), put \\(\\alpha_s=\\lambda e^{\\lambda s}\\), and let \\(H\\) be a sufficiently large positive integer. Put \\(P=\\lfloor\\log\\log H\\rfloor\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A tail witness is\n  \\[\n  \\eta=((Q_h)_{P\\le h&lt;H},a),\n  \\]\n  where the \\(Q_h\\) are primes and \\(a\\ge1\\) is an integer. Writing \\(v_h=\\log_2Q_h\\), require\n  \\[\n  0.9\\alpha_sh\\rho^{-h}\\le v_h\\le1.1\\alpha_sh\\rho^{-h}\n  \\quad(P\\le h&lt;H),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{l=P}^{h-1}a_{h-l}v_l\n  \\le(1+10^{-4}e^{-h/40})v_h\n  \\quad(P\\le h&lt;H), \\tag{2.5}\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  P^+(a)\\le Q_P,\\qquad\n  \\log a\\le\\exp(2\\alpha_sP\\rho^{-P}).\n  \\]\n  Here \\(P^+(a)\\) is the largest prime factor of \\(a\\), with \\(P^+(1)=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  w(\\eta)=a\\prod_{h=P}^{H-1}Q_h,\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal W_{H,s}(d)=\\{\\eta:\\varphi(w(\\eta))=d\\},\n  \\]\n  and, for \\(h\\ge H\\),\n  \\[\n  D(h,\\eta)=\\sum_{l=P}^{H-1}a_{h-l}v_l. \\tag{2.6}\n  \\]\n  For fixed \\(H\\), the universe of possible witnesses, over all \\(s\\in[0,1)\\), is finite.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_h\\), \\(h\\ge H\\), be independent mean-one exponential random variables, and put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal E_\\eta=\n  \\bigcap_{h\\ge H}\n  \\left\\{E_h\\ge\\frac{\\gamma}{\\alpha_s}\\rho^hD(h,\\eta)\\right\\}. \\tag{2.7}\n  \\]\n  For \\(f:[1,\\infty)\\to[0,1]\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  A_H(f;s)\n  &amp;=\n  \\rho^{H(H-1)/2}\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{\\alpha_s}\\right)^H\n  \\sum_{\\substack{d\\in\\mathcal V\\\\\\mathcal W_{H,s}(d)\\ne\\varnothing}}\n  \\frac{f(\\ell(d)/d)}{d}\n  \\Pr\\left(\\bigcup_{\\eta\\in\\mathcal W_{H,s}(d)}\\mathcal E_\\eta\\right)\\\\\n  &amp;=\n  \\rho^{H(H-1)/2}\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{\\alpha_s}\\right)^H\n  \\sum_{\\substack{d\\in\\mathcal V\\\\\\mathcal W_{H,s}(d)\\ne\\varnothing}}\n  \\frac{f(\\ell(d)/d)}{d}\n  \\sum_{\\varnothing\\ne T\\subseteq\\mathcal W_{H,s}(d)}\n  (-1)^{|T|-1}\n  \\exp\\left\\{\n  -\\frac{\\gamma}{\\alpha_s}\n  \\sum_{h=H}^{\\infty}\\rho^h\\max_{\\eta\\in T}D(h,\\eta)\n  \\right\\}. \\qquad(2.8)\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  The series converge absolutely. For \\(f=1\\), no least-preimage search occurs. For other weights, any witness supplies the finite bound \\(\\ell(d)\\le w(\\eta)\\). When the limit exists, write\n  \\[\n  A(f;s)=\\lim_{H\\to\\infty}A_H(f;s).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The nonnegative functions \\(A_H(1;\\cdot)\\) converge uniformly on \\([0,1)\\) to a function \\(A(1;\\cdot)\\) satisfying\n\\[\n0&lt;\\inf_{0\\le s&lt;1}A(1;s)\n\\le\\sup_{0\\le s&lt;1}A(1;s)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nV(x)\\sim\\frac{x}{\\log x}G_mA(1;\\theta),\n\\]\nand, for every fixed real \\(c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{V(cx)}{V(x)}\\longrightarrow c.\n\\]\nNo continuity of \\(s\\mapsto A(1;s)\\) is asserted or required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set\n\\[\nC_1(x)=\\frac{V(x)\\log x}{xG_m}.\n\\]\nProposition 10 at \\(t=x\\) gives the common-comparison hypothesis (6.13) with \\(f=1\\). Propositions 13–14 therefore prove uniform existence of \\(A(1;s)\\) and\n\\[\nC_1(x)=A(1;\\theta(x))+o(1).\n\\]\nProposition 1 supplies positive absolute constants \\(c_-,c_+\\) bounding \\(C_1(x)\\). The bounds clause of Proposition 14 gives\n\\[\n0&lt;c_-\\le A(1;s)\\le c_+&lt;\\infty\n\\quad(0\\le s&lt;1).\n\\]\nThe additive approximation is consequently equivalent to\n\\[\nV(x)\\sim\\frac x{\\log x}G_mA(1;\\theta(x)).\n\\]</p><p>The coefficient was defined by the finite arithmetic formula (2.8). The count \\(V\\) establishes convergence and bounds, rather than defining the coefficient.</p><p>Proposition 11 gives\n\\[\nV(cx)/V(x)\\to c\n\\]\nfor every fixed \\(c&gt;0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 2.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Definitions and conditions.", "statement": "<p>Definitions and conditions.\nUse \\(\\mathcal V,V,\\ell,\\rho,\\lambda,\\gamma,B,m,\\theta,G_m,A_H\\), and \\(A\\) as defined in Theorem 1. Fix a positive integer \\(k\\), and define\n\\[\nN_k(x)=\\#\\{v\\in\\mathcal V:v\\le x,\\ kx&lt;\\ell(v)\\le(k+1)x\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nf_k(r)=\\min\\{1,(k+1)/r\\}-\\min\\{1,k/r\\}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\nEquivalently,\n\\[\nf_k(r)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&amp;1\\le r\\le k,\\\\\n1-k/r,&amp;k&lt;r&lt;k+1,\\\\\n1/r,&amp;r\\ge k+1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThus \\(0\\le f_k\\le1/(k+1)\\), and \\(f_k(r)&gt;0\\) exactly when \\(r&gt;k\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functions \\(A_H(f_k;\\cdot)\\) converge uniformly on \\([0,1)\\), and\n\\[\nN_k(x)=\\frac{x}{\\log x}G_m\\bigl(A(f_k;\\theta)+o(1)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThere are two alternatives:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If some \\(d\\in\\mathcal V\\) satisfies \\(\\ell(d)&gt;kd\\), then\n   \\[\n   \\inf_{0\\le s&lt;1}A(f_k;s)&gt;0,\n   \\qquad\n   N_k(x)\\sim\\frac{x}{\\log x}G_mA(f_k;\\theta)\\asymp_kV(x).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If no such \\(d\\) exists, then \\(N_k(x)=0\\) for every \\(x&gt;0\\), and \\(A(f_k;s)=0\\) for every \\(s\\in[0,1)\\).</p><p>The first alternative holds for \\(k=1,2\\). The theorem does not determine which other integers \\(k\\) satisfy the seed condition. Its occurrence for every positive integer \\(k\\) is equivalent to the unboundedness of \\(\\ell(d)/d\\) over totients \\(d\\); that unboundedness is not established.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nC_{f_k}(x)=\\frac{N_k(x)\\log x}{xG_m}.\n\\]\nThis function is independent of \\(H\\). Proposition 15 supplies the common-comparison hypothesis of Proposition 14. Hence \\(A_H(f_k;\\cdot)\\) converges uniformly on \\([0,1)\\), and\n\\[\nN_k(x)\n=\n\\frac{xG_m}{\\log x}\n\\bigl(A(f_k;\\theta)+o(1)\\bigr). \\tag{7.4}\n\\]</p><p>If a seed \\(d_*\\) satisfies \\(\\ell(d_*)&gt;kd_*\\), Proposition 16 bounds \\(C_{f_k}(x)\\) above and away from zero for all sufficiently large \\(x\\). The bounds clause of Proposition 14 yields\n\\[\n\\inf_{0\\le s&lt;1}A(f_k;s)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThus (7.4) becomes a multiplicative asymptotic. Its positive lower bound, \\(N_k(x)\\le V(x)\\), and Proposition 1 give\n\\[\nN_k(x)\\asymp_kV(x).\n\\]</p><p>If there is no seed, Proposition 16 gives \\(N_k(x)=0\\) and \\(A_H(f_k;s)=0\\), so the limit coefficient is identically zero. Proposition 16 also establishes the positive alternative for \\(k=1,2\\).</p><p>The argument does not classify the remaining integers \\(k\\). The first alternative for every positive integer \\(k\\) is equivalent to unboundedness of \\(\\ell(d)/d\\), which the paper leaves unresolved.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "equations (3.4)–(3.6) and Lemma 3.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions and definitions.\nUse the constants and scale of Theorem 1. For \\(1\\le j\\le m\\), fix numbers \\(\\beta_i\\ge1\\), \\(0\\le i&lt;j\\), and put \\(\\beta_j=1\\). With \\(u_0=B\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_j(\\boldsymbol\\beta)\n=\n\\left\\{(u_1,\\ldots,u_j):\ns_i:=\\beta_iu_i-\\sum_{r=i+1}^ja_{r-i}u_r\\ge0\n\\quad(0\\le i\\le j)\\right\\}.\n\\]\nFor \\(j=0\\), the simplex consists of the empty tuple and has volume \\(G_0=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Ford’s estimates give\n\\[\nV(x)\\asymp\\frac{x}{\\log x}G_m, \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\\[\ng_j=\\gamma\\rho^{-j}+O(1),\\qquad g_j\\asymp\\rho^{-j}, \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{G_j}{G_m}\n=\n\\prod_{i=j+1}^m\\frac{ig_i}{B}\n\\le C^{m-j}\\rho^{(m-j)(m-j-1)/2}\\ll1\n\\quad(0\\le j\\le m). \\tag{3.6}\n\\]</p><p>The true simplex \\(\\mathcal S_j(\\mathbf1)\\) has volume \\(G_j\\). Under its uniform volume measure,\n\\[\nW_i=\\frac{g_is_i}{B}\n\\]\nare uniform on\n\\[\n\\{W_i\\ge0:\\textstyle\\sum_{i=0}^jW_i=1\\}.\n\\]\nFurthermore,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Vol}\\mathcal S_j(\\boldsymbol\\beta)\n\\le G_j\\prod_{r&lt;j}\\beta_r^{j-r}.\n\\]\nWithin \\(\\mathcal S_j(\\boldsymbol\\beta)\\), a specified slack \\(s_t\\) in an interval of length \\(D\\) occupies at most a fraction \\(jg_tD/B\\) of the volume. A specified coordinate \\(u_t\\), \\(1\\le t\\le j\\), in such an interval occupies at most a fraction \\(jg_t\\beta_tD/B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The scale estimates are Ford [7, Theorem 1, Lemmas 3.4 and 3.7, and Corollary 3.5]; Ford denotes the present constant \\(\\gamma\\) by \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p>For the true simplex, triangular inversion gives\n\\[\nu_i=\\sum_{t=i}^jg_{t-i}s_t,\\qquad\nB=\\sum_{t=0}^jg_ts_t.\n\\]\nThe transformation from \\(u_1,\\ldots,u_j\\) to \\(s_1,\\ldots,s_j\\) has determinant \\(1\\). This proves the volume and distribution statements.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nK_i=\\prod_{r&lt;i}\\beta_r,\\qquad K_0=1.\n\\]\nThe map \\(u_i\\mapsto u_i/K_i\\) sends \\(\\mathcal S_j(\\boldsymbol\\beta)\\) into \\(\\mathcal S_j(\\mathbf1)\\), because \\(K_r\\ge\\beta_iK_i\\) when \\(r&gt;i\\). Its inverse Jacobian is \\(\\prod_{r&lt;j}\\beta_r^{j-r}\\).</p><p>For the slice estimates, define\n\\[\n\\gamma'_0=1,\\qquad\n\\gamma'_t=\\beta_t^{-1}\\sum_{i&lt;t}\\gamma'_ia_{t-i}.\n\\]\nThen \\(0&lt;\\gamma'_t\\le g_t\\) and\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=0}^j\\gamma'_ts_t=\\beta_0B.\n\\]\nThe marginal density of \\(s_t\\) under uniform measure is at most \\(j\\gamma'_t/(\\beta_0B)\\). For the coordinate assertion, fix all other positive-index slacks. The coordinate \\(u_t\\) is affine in \\(s_t\\) with slope \\(1/\\beta_t\\). Integrating first in this fiber and bounding by the full projection volume gives the additional factor \\(\\beta_t\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemma 3.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions and definitions.\nUse \\(B,m,\\rho,G_j,\\mathcal S_j(\\mathbf1)\\) from Theorem 1 and Proposition 1. Put\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\frac{B\\rho^m}{m},\\qquad\nh_i=m-i,\\qquad\nb_i=\\alpha h_i\\rho^{-h_i}.\n\\]\nFix \\(\\eta&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are constants \\(C_\\eta,c_\\eta&gt;0\\) such that, if \\(0\\le Q&lt;m\\) is an integer, \\(j=m-Q\\), \\(1\\le i\\le j\\), and \\(h_i\\ge C_\\eta(Q+1)\\), then uniform volume measure on \\(\\mathcal S_j(\\mathbf1)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\Pr(|u_i/b_i-1|&gt;\\eta)\\le C_\\eta e^{-c_\\eta h_i}.\n\\]\nAlso, for \\(T\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\Pr(u_j\\ge T)\n=\n(1-g_jT/B)_+^j\n\\le e^{-jg_jT/B},\n\\qquad z_+=\\max\\{z,0\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 1,\n\\[\n\\frac{u_i}{B\\rho^i}\n=\n\\sum_{t=i}^j(1+O(\\rho^{t-i}))W_t.\n\\]\nRepresent\n\\[\nW_t=\\frac{E_t}{\\sum_{r=0}^jE_r},\n\\]\nwhere the \\(E_t\\) are independent mean-one exponential variables. The main numerator has\n\\[\nj-i+1=h_i-Q+1\n\\]\nterms, and\n\\[\n\\frac{(j-i+1)/(j+1)}{h_i/m}\n=\n1+O((Q+1)/h_i).\n\\]\nExponential moment bounds give exponentially small probabilities for a fixed relative deviation of either the numerator or the denominator. The error numerator is bounded in absolute value by\n\\[\nC\\sum_{r\\ge0}\\rho^rE_{i+r};\n\\]\nthis sum has a uniformly bounded exponential moment, since\n\\[\n\\prod_{r\\ge0}(1-z\\rho^r)^{-1}&lt;\\infty\n\\quad(0&lt;z&lt;1).\n\\]\nOnce \\(h_i\\ge C_\\eta(Q+1)\\), these three bounds prove the first assertion. The second is the exact marginal tail of \\(W_j\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 3.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions and definitions.\nUse the scale of Theorem 1. Fix a sufficiently large integer \\(H\\), and put\n\\[\nP=\\lfloor\\log\\log H\\rfloor,\\qquad R=m-H,\\qquad L=m-P. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nFirst let \\(x\\to\\infty\\), then \\(H\\to\\infty\\). Write\n\\[\nh_i=m-i,\\qquad\n\\alpha=\\frac{B\\rho^m}{m},\\qquad\nb_i=\\alpha h_i\\rho^{-h_i},\\qquad\n\\widetilde b_i=\\alpha_\\theta h_i\\rho^{-h_i},\n\\qquad\n\\xi_i=1+10^{-4}e^{-h_i/40}.\n\\]\nIn particular \\(b_0=B\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{\\alpha}{\\alpha_\\theta}\n=\n\\frac{\\log B}{\\lambda m}\n=\n1+\\frac{\\lambda\\theta+\\log\\log B}{\\lambda m}\n=\n1+O\\left(\\frac{\\log m}{m}\\right). \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nThe coarse numerical bounds used below are\n\\[\n0.54&lt;\\rho&lt;0.545. \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nThe simplexes \\(\\mathcal S_j\\) are as in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(0\\le j\\le L\\), sum over primes \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_j\\) whose coordinates \\(u_i=\\log_2p_i\\) satisfy\n\\[\n0.9\\widetilde b_i\\le u_i\\le1.1\\widetilde b_i,\n\\qquad\n(u_1,\\ldots,u_j)\\in\\mathcal S_j(\\boldsymbol\\xi).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\sum_{p_1,\\ldots,p_j}\\prod_{i=1}^j\\frac1{p_i-1}\\ll G_m. \\tag{3.13}\n\\]\nEvery unit box meeting this region lies in \\(\\mathcal S_j(\\boldsymbol\\beta)\\), where\n\\[\n\\beta_t=1+C(e^{-h_t/40}+h_t^2\\rho^{h_t})\n\\quad(0\\le t&lt;j),\\qquad\n\\beta_j=1, \\tag{3.14}\n\\]\nfor a sufficiently large absolute \\(C\\). This enlargement has bounded volume cost. Its coordinate scaling factors through \\(R\\) differ from \\(1\\) by\n\\[\nO(e^{-H/40}+H^2\\rho^H).\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(A&gt;0\\). Among unit boxes meeting the region in \\(L\\) coordinates, retain those with a point satisfying, for some \\(i\\le R\\), at least one of:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(i\\ge1\\) and \\(u_i\\) is within \\(A\\) of a coarse band endpoint;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(\\left|\\xi_iu_i-\\sum_{r=i+1}^La_{r-i}u_r\\right|\\le Ah_i^2\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(\\sum_{r=i+1}^La_{r-i}u_r\\ge(1-h_i^{-4})u_i\\).</p><p>Their total volume and total reciprocal-prime weight are\n\\[\nO_A(H^{-2}G_m). \\tag{3.15}\n\\]\nThe same bound holds when any subset of coordinates uses reciprocal-prime measure and the others use Lebesgue measure. For conditions 1–2 alone, or for the region lost by replacing \\(\\xi_i\\), \\(0\\le i\\le R\\), by \\(1\\), the stronger bound is\n\\[\nO_A\\left(G_m\\sum_{h\\ge H}(he^{-h/40}+h^3\\rho^h)\\right).\n\\]\nAll estimates are uniform in the phase.</p>", "proof": "<p>Mertens’ theorem gives, for \\(b\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{b\\le\\log_2p&lt;b+1}\\frac1{p-1}\n=\n1+O(e^{-b}). \\tag{3.12}\n\\]\nReplacing \\(1/p\\) by \\(1/(p-1)\\) changes the sum by \\(O(\\exp(-\\exp b))\\). When the lower endpoints grow geometrically backwards, multiplying these estimates costs a bounded factor; if the sum of their errors is small, it is the relative product error.</p><p>The band lower bounds are \\(u_t\\gg h_t\\rho^{-h_t}\\). Changing each coordinate by at most \\(1\\) changes inequality \\(t\\) by \\(O(h_t^2)\\). These errors are absorbed by (3.14), including at \\(t=0\\), where \\(u_0=B\\) is fixed. The logarithm of the Jacobian upper bound is at most\n\\[\nC\\sum_{h\\ge P}h(e^{-h/40}+h^2\\rho^h)\\ll1.\n\\]\nProposition 1 and (3.12) prove (3.13). The coordinate scaling factor is \\(\\prod_{t&lt;i}\\beta_t\\), without the Jacobian exponents; summing for \\(h_t&gt;h_i\\ge H\\) gives its stated bound.</p><p>For the shells, put \\(h=h_i\\). Throughout a box meeting the bands, \\(u_i\\ll b_i\\). Conditions 2–3, together with unit perturbations, put the enlarged slack in an interval starting at zero of length at most\n\\[\nC_A\\{b_i(h^{-4}+e^{-h/40})+h^3\\}.\n\\]\nThe \\(h^3\\) term includes \\(h^2\\rho^hu_i=O(h^3)\\). Since \\(Lg_i/B\\ll\\rho^h\\), Proposition 1 bounds the relative volume by\n\\[\nC_A(h^{-3}+he^{-h/40}+h^3\\rho^h).\n\\]\nCondition 1, after enlarging its interval by \\(2\\), costs \\(O_A(\\rho^h)\\). Sum over \\(h\\ge H\\), using the bounded enlargement cost. The prime-weight assertion follows from (3.12).</p><p>For the stronger assertion, omit \\(h^{-4}\\) from the slack interval. Replacing \\(\\xi_i\\) by \\(1\\) removes only points whose old slack lies between \\(0\\) and \\((\\xi_i-1)u_i\\), which obeys the same bound.</p><p>Finally, every full unit box has uniformly bounded mass when an arbitrary subset of its coordinates uses reciprocal-prime measure: multiply the factors in (3.12) for precisely that subset. This proves the mixed-measure assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 3.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions and definitions.\nFor \\(a\\ge1\\), let \\(P^+(a)\\) denote its largest prime factor, with \\(P^+(1)=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly for \\(Y\\ge\\exp(\\exp2)\\) and \\(Z\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{P^+(a)\\le Y}\\frac{a^{1/\\log Y}}{\\varphi(a)}\\ll\\log Y,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{P^+(a)\\le Y\\\\\\log a&gt;Z}}\\frac1{\\varphi(a)}\n\\ll(\\log Y)\\exp(-Z/\\log Y).\n\\]\nIn the setting of Proposition 3, put\n\\[\nK_H=\\exp(CP\\rho^{-P})=H^{o(1)},\n\\]\nallowing the absolute \\(C\\) to increase in upper bounds. With\n\\[\nY=\\exp\\exp(1.1\\widetilde b_L),\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\sum_{P^+(a)\\le Y}\\frac1{\\varphi(a)}\\le K_H,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{P^+(a)\\le Y\\\\\\log a&gt;\\exp(2\\widetilde b_L)}}\n\\frac1{\\varphi(a)}\n\\le\nK_H\\exp\\{-\\exp(cP\\rho^{-P})\\}. \\tag{3.16}\n\\]\nConsequently the shell estimates of Proposition 3, after summing a smooth cofactor with weight \\(1/\\varphi(a)\\), have weighted mass at most\n\\[\nCK_HG_m\n\\left\\{\nH^{-2}+\\sum_{h\\ge H}(he^{-h/40}+h^3\\rho^h)\n\\right\\}. \\tag{3.17}\n\\]\nThe \\(H^{-2}\\) term is needed only for condition 3 of Proposition 3.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\sigma=1/\\log Y\\). The Euler factor is\n\\[\n1+\\frac{p^\\sigma}{(p-1)(1-p^{\\sigma-1})},\n\\]\nwhose logarithm is\n\\[\np^{-1+\\sigma}+O(p^{-2+2\\sigma})\n\\]\nuniformly in this range. Partial summation gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{p\\le Y}(p^{-1+\\sigma}-p^{-1})=O(1);\n\\]\nhence the product is \\(O(\\log Y)\\). Rankin’s inequality gives the tail bound.</p><p>For (3.16), the exponent is\n\\[\n1.1\\widetilde b_L-\\exp(0.9\\widetilde b_L),\n\\]\nand \\(\\widetilde b_L\\asymp P\\rho^{-P}\\). Since \\(\\lambda&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\exp(CP\\rho^{-P})=H^{o(1)}.\n\\]\nCombining these bounds with Proposition 3 gives (3.17).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 3.3; the witness definition is Definition 3.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions and definitions.\nUse the setting and notation of Proposition 3. A basic witness is a choice of primes \\(p_0,\\ldots,p_L\\) and an integer \\(a\\ge1\\) satisfying\n\\[\np_0\\ge x^{0.9},\\qquad\n0.9\\widetilde b_i\\le\\log_2p_i\\le1.1\\widetilde b_i\n\\quad(1\\le i\\le L),\n\\]\n\\[\n(u_1,\\ldots,u_L)\\in\\mathcal S_L(\\boldsymbol\\xi),\n\\qquad\nP^+(a)\\le p_L,\\qquad\n\\log a\\le\\exp(2\\widetilde b_L), \\tag{3.10}\n\\]\nwhere \\(u_0=B\\) and \\(u_i=\\log_2p_i\\) for \\(i\\ge1\\). The prime bands are disjoint and decreasing, so the displayed prefix is strictly decreasing. Repetitions of \\(p_L\\) inside \\(a\\) are allowed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a set \\(\\mathcal E_x\\subseteq\\mathcal V\\cap[1,x]\\) such that\n\\[\n|\\mathcal E_x|\\le\\{\\varepsilon_H+o_{x;H}(1)\\}V(x),\n\\]\nand every preimage of every\n\\[\nv\\in(\\mathcal V\\cap[1,x])\\setminus\\mathcal E_x\n\\]\nhas the form \\(p_0\\cdots p_La\\) for a basic witness. One may take\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_H\\ll\ne^{-cP}+K_H\\exp\\{-\\exp(cP\\rho^{-P})\\}.\n\\]\nFor every basic witness, with \\(w=p_{R+1}\\cdots p_La\\),\n\\[\np_1\\cdots p_La\\le\\exp((\\log x)^{0.8}),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log w\\le H\\exp(0.62b_R)+\\exp(2\\widetilde b_L),\n\\qquad w&lt;p_R. \\tag{3.11}\n\\]\nIn particular \\(w\\) is bounded in terms of \\(H\\) alone, uniformly in phase.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take \\(\\eta=0.04\\) in Lemma 5.1. By (3.2), its complementary bands imply the coarse bands in (3.10). Apply (3.8) as well. Both conclusions hold for every preimage outside the union of their exceptional sets.</p><p>Remove also \\(v\\le x^{0.95}\\) and \\(\\Omega(v)&gt;5B\\), at cost \\(o(V(x))\\). For the latter, the reciprocal moment bound with \\(z=1.9&lt;2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\#\\{v\\le x:\\Omega(v)&gt;5B\\}\n\\le\nz^{-5B}x\\sum_{r\\le x}\\frac{z^{\\Omega(r)}}r\n\\ll x\\exp((z-5\\log z)B)\n=o(x/\\log x).\n\\]\nThe Euler product bounds the reciprocal sum by \\(O((\\log x)^z)\\), and \\(z-5\\log z&lt;-1\\).</p><p>For every preimage,\n\\[\n\\Omega(n)\\le\\Omega(\\varphi(n))+1\\le6B.\n\\]\nThe \\(p_1\\) band gives \\(\\log p_1\\le(\\log x)^{0.61}\\), so\n\\[\n\\log p_0\n\\ge0.95\\log x-6B(\\log x)^{0.61}\n&gt;0.9\\log x.\n\\]</p><p>It remains to impose the size restriction on \\(a\\). For a preimage failing it, fix \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_L,a\\). The prime \\(p_0\\) is distinct from the others and from the factors of \\(a\\). Supermultiplicativity gives\n\\[\n\\varphi(p_0\\cdots p_La)\n\\ge(p_0-1)\\varphi(a)\\prod_{i=1}^L(p_i-1).\n\\]\nThe prime number theorem upper bound therefore gives at most\n\\[\n\\frac{Cx}{\\log x}\n\\frac1{\\varphi(a)\\prod_{i=1}^L(p_i-1)}\n\\]\nchoices of \\(p_0\\); if the interval is nonempty, its upper endpoint is at least \\(x^{0.9}\\). Sum the prefix by Proposition 3 and the cofactor by Proposition 4. This bounds all failing preimages and hence all values with at least one failing preimage.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{i+1}}{b_i}=(1-1/h_i)\\rho.\n\\]\nThe bands and (3.2) imply that the largest double logarithm after index \\(R\\) is at most \\(0.62b_R\\). There are at most \\(H\\) such primes, giving the bound for \\(\\log w\\). Its right side is smaller than\n\\[\n\\exp(0.89b_R)&lt;\\log p_R\n\\]\nfor large \\(H\\), then large \\(x\\). The first line of (3.11) follows from the \\(p_1\\) bound, \\(L\\ll\\log B\\), and the fact that the \\(a\\)-bound depends only on \\(H\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Ford structure estimates (3.7)–(3.8)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Conditions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions.\nUse the setting of Proposition 5. Order the prime factors of a preimage \\(n\\), with repetitions, as \\(p_0\\ge p_1\\ge\\cdots\\). A missing indicated prime is a failure of the stated condition.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(0&lt;\\eta\\le1/8\\),\n\\[\n\\#\\left\\{\nv\\le x:\n\\begin{array}{l}\nv\\in\\mathcal V,\\ \\text{some preimage has}\\\\\n|\\log_2p_i/b_i-1|&gt;\\eta\n\\text{ for some }1\\le i\\le m-P\n\\end{array}\n\\right\\}\n\\ll_\\eta V(x)e^{-c_\\eta P}. \\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nAlso, all but \\(O(V(x)e^{-P^2/4})\\) values have every preimage satisfying\n\\[\n\\log_2p_L&gt;2,\\qquad\n\\sum_{r=i+1}^La_{r-i}u_r\\le\\xi_iu_i\n\\quad(0\\le i&lt;L),\n\\]\n\\[\nu_0=B,\\qquad u_i=\\log_2p_i\\quad(i\\ge1). \\tag{3.8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Ford [7, Theorem 10], together with the missing-prime exception in Theorem 16. For \\(i\\le3\\eta m\\), Theorem 10(b) contributes at most\n\\[\nCme^{-\\eta m/13}V(x).\n\\]\nFor \\(i&gt;3\\eta m\\), Theorem 10(a) contributes at most\n\\[\nCV(x)\\frac{i}{\\eta m}\n\\exp\\left\\{-\\frac{\\eta^2m(m-i)}{4i}\\right\\}\n\\le\n\\frac{CV(x)}{\\eta}e^{-\\eta^2(m-i)/4}.\n\\]\nSum over \\(m-i\\ge P\\). A missing \\(p_L\\) is included in the \\(O(V(x)e^{-P^2/4})\\) exception of Theorem 16. The convention preceding Ford’s Theorem 10 counts values with some failing preimage, so the complement controls every preimage.</p><p>Ford’s Theorem 16, with parameter \\(\\Psi=P\\), gives (3.8). Its final adjacent-coordinate inequality has coefficient \\(1\\) in place of \\(a_1&lt;1\\); weakening that inequality gives the displayed version.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 3.8; normality is Definition 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions and definitions.\nUse the basic-witness setting of Proposition 5. Fix \\(c&gt;1\\), let \\(t=x\\) or \\(t=x/c\\), and retain all parameters formed from \\(x\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal T(t)\\) be the set of distinct tuples\n\\[\n\\tau=(p_0,\\ldots,p_R,d)\n\\]\nadmitting at least one basic witness with\n\\[\nw=p_{R+1}\\cdots p_La,\\qquad d=\\varphi(w),\n\\qquad\nd\\prod_{i=0}^R(p_i-1)\\le t.\n\\]\nEach tuple is counted once, regardless of its number of witnesses.</p><p>For integers \\(n\\), let \\(\\Omega(n,U,T)\\) count prime factors in \\((U,T]\\), with multiplicity, and put \\(\\Omega(n)=\\Omega(n,1,n)\\), including \\(\\Omega(1)=0\\). A prime \\(p\\) is \\(S\\)-normal, for \\(S\\ge e^e\\), if\n\\[\n\\Omega(p-1,1,S)\\le2\\log_2S\n\\]\nand, for every \\(S\\le U&lt;T\\le p-1\\),\n\\[\n|\\Omega(p-1,U,T)-(\\log_2T-\\log_2U)|\n&lt;\n\\sqrt{\\log_2S\\,\\log_2T}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(0\\le i\\le R\\), put\n\\[\nJ_i=\\lceil30\\log h_i\\rceil,\\qquad\nS_i=\\exp\\exp(b_i^{1/3}),\\qquad\nz_i=\\exp\\exp(0.7b_{i+J_i}),\n\\]\nand, for a basic witness,\n\\[\nD_i=\\varphi(p_{i+J_i+1}\\cdots p_La).\n\\]\nFor large \\(H\\), \\(J_i&lt;h_i/2\\) and \\(i+J_i&lt;L\\).</p><p>A tuple is good if every basic witness giving it satisfies, for every \\(0\\le i\\le R\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{r=i+1}^La_{r-i}u_r\\le(1-h_i^{-4})u_i,\n\\]\n\\[\np_i,\\ldots,p_{i+J_i}\\text{ are }S_i\\text{-normal},\n\\]\n\\[\n(p_i-1)\\cdots(p_{i+J_i}-1)\n\\text{ is squarefree on primes exceeding }z_i,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Omega(D_i)\\le b_i/h_i^8. \\tag{3.18}\n\\]\nThe first condition uses \\(u_0=B\\). Write\n\\[\n\\mathcal G(t)=\\{\\text{good tuples in }\\mathcal T(t)\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal D(t)=\\mathcal T(t)\\setminus\\mathcal G(t).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly for the two endpoints,\n\\[\n|\\mathcal D(t)|\n\\le\\{\\varepsilon_H+o_{x;H}(1)\\}\\frac{xG_m}{\\log x},\n\\qquad\n\\varepsilon_H\\ll K_HH^{-2}.\n\\]\nFor all the witnesses under consideration,\n\\[\nP^+(D_i)&lt;z_i,\\qquad S_i&lt;z_i,\\qquad\nch_i^{-20}\\le b_{i+J_i}/b_i\\le Ch_i^{-18}. \\tag{3.19}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The last comparison follows from\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{i+J_i}}{b_i}=(1-J_i/h_i)\\rho^{J_i},\n\\qquad18&lt;30\\log(1/\\rho)&lt;20.\n\\]\nIt implies \\(S_i&lt;z_i\\). The next prime band has double-logarithmic upper endpoint below \\(0.7b_{i+J_i}\\); all remaining primes, including those in \\(a\\), are smaller. Taking totients cannot introduce a prime above this bound.</p><p>A bad tuple has a failing full witness. Bound these witnesses, which bounds the number of bad tuples regardless of witness multiplicity. Counting \\(p_0\\) gives \\(Cx/\\log x\\), with reciprocal factors for the other variables; the smooth \\(a\\)-sum costs \\(K_H\\). Failure of the first condition in (3.18) is covered by Proposition 3, condition 3, and contributes\n\\[\nO(K_HH^{-2}xG_m/\\log x).\n\\]</p><p>For the other conditions fix \\(i\\), and write \\(h=h_i\\), \\(b=b_i\\), \\(J=J_i\\). If \\(i\\ge1\\), the reciprocal sum for \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_{i-1}\\) is \\(O(G_m)\\). All \\(p_i,\\ldots,p_L\\) are at most \\(\\exp\\exp(1.2b)\\); their unrestricted reciprocal sums cost \\(\\exp(Ch^2)\\), since there are at most \\(h\\) variables and \\(\\log b=O(h)\\). For \\(i=0\\), omit \\(p_0\\) and use the same estimate at \\(b=B\\).</p><p>Ford [7, Lemma 2.6] gives\n\\[\n\\#\\{p\\le z:p\\text{ is not }S\\text{-normal}\\}\n\\ll\\frac z{\\log z}(\\log_2z)^5(\\log S)^{-1/6}. \\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nPartial summation gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{p\\le\\exp\\exp(1.2b)\\\\p\\text{ not }S_i\\text{-normal}}}\n\\frac1{p-1}\n\\ll(1+b)^6e^{-b^{1/3}/6}\n\\le e^{-cb^{1/3}}.\n\\]\nFor a failure involving \\(p_0\\), apply (3.9) directly to its counting interval, whose upper endpoint has logarithm comparable with \\(\\log x\\). Relative to \\(xG_m/\\log x\\), normality failures contribute at most\n\\[\nK_H\\sum_{h\\ge H}\\exp(Ch^2-cb(h)^{1/3}),\n\\qquad b(h)=\\alpha h\\rho^{-h}. \\tag{3.20}\n\\]</p><p>A square of a prime \\(q&gt;z_i\\) either divides one shift twice or divides two different shifts. For \\(\\ell=1,2\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{p\\le Z\\\\q^\\ell\\mid p-1}}\\frac1{p-1}\n\\le\\frac{1+\\log Z}{q^\\ell},\n\\]\nby summing over positive multiples of \\(q^\\ell\\). If the shift is \\(p_0-1\\le T\\), its integer count is at most \\(T/q^\\ell\\). Relative to prime counting, this loses at most \\(\\log x=e^b\\) when \\(i=0\\). Both kinds of square cost \\(q^{-2}\\), up to \\(\\exp(Cb+Ch^2)\\). Their total relative contribution is at most\n\\[\nK_H\\sum_{h\\ge H}\n\\exp\\{Ch^2+Cb(h)-\\exp(cb(h)h^{-20})\\}. \\tag{3.21}\n\\]</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\Omega(\\varphi(a))\\ll\\log a\n\\le e^{2\\widetilde b_L}=H^{o(1)},\n\\]\nwhereas \\(b_i/h_i^8\\) is exponentially large in \\(h_i\\ge H\\). Overlap between \\(a\\) and displayed primes occurs only at \\(p_L\\); it changes the sum of factor counts by \\(1-\\Omega(p_L-1)\\le0\\). Failure of the final condition in (3.18) forces some \\(i+J&lt;j\\le L\\) to satisfy\n\\[\n\\Omega(p_j-1)&gt;b_i/(2h^9).\n\\]\nThe Euler product bound\n\\[\n\\sum_{n\\le Z}\\frac{(3/2)^{\\Omega(n)}}n\\ll(\\log Z)^{3/2}\n\\]\nand the band bound imply that the exceptional reciprocal sum is at most\n\\[\n\\exp(Cb_{i+J}-cb_i/h^9)\n\\le\\exp(-c'b_i/h^9),\n\\]\nbecause \\(b_{i+J}\\ll b_ih^{-18}\\). The resulting relative contribution is at most\n\\[\nK_H\\sum_{h\\ge H}\\exp(Ch^2-cb(h)/h^9). \\tag{3.22}\n\\]\nEach sum in (3.20)–(3.22) decreases faster than every inverse power of \\(H\\), uniformly in phase. Together with the thin-slack bound, this proves the proposition. Replacing \\(x\\) by \\(x/c\\) only decreases the witness sets.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 4.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions and definitions.\nLet \\(y\\ge y_0\\), put \\(B_y=\\log_2y\\), and let \\(1\\le b\\le B_y\\) be an integer. Assume\n\\[\nY_0=y,\\qquad e^e\\le S\\le Y_b,\\qquad\nY_{j+1}&lt;U_j&lt;Y_j\\quad(0\\le j&lt;b).\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\n\\nu_j=\\frac{\\log_2Y_j}{B_y}\\quad(0\\le j\\le b),\\qquad\n\\mu_j=\\frac{\\log_2U_j}{B_y}\\quad(0\\le j&lt;b),\n\\qquad\n\\delta=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log_2S}{B_y}}.\n\\]\nFix an integer \\(D\\ge1\\) with \\(P^+(D)\\le Y_b\\), and a real \\(r\\ge1\\).</p><p>Let \\(N\\) count ordered tuples\n\\[\n(p_0,\\ldots,p_{b-1},q_0,\\ldots,q_{b-1},D')\n\\]\nsuch that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(D'\\ge1\\) is an integer; the \\(p_j,q_j\\) are \\(S\\)-normal primes, \\(p_j\\ne q_j\\), and\n   \\[\n   U_j\\le P^+(p_j-1),P^+(q_j-1)\\le Y_j;\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(P^+(D')\\le Y_b\\) and\n   \\[\n   D\\prod_{j=0}^{b-1}(p_j-1)\n   =\n   D'\\prod_{j=0}^{b-1}(q_j-1)\n   \\le y/r;\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. the common product is squarefree on primes greater than \\(Y_b\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">4. the part of \\(p_0-1\\) supported above \\(Y_1\\) exceeds \\(\\sqrt y\\).</p><p>Normality is as defined in Proposition 6. The part of an integer supported above \\(Z\\) is the product of its exact prime powers whose primes exceed \\(Z\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are absolute constants \\(C,y_0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN\\le{}&amp;\n\\frac{y}{rD}(CB_y^6)^b(b+1)^{\\Omega(D)}\n(\\log Y_b)^{1+3b\\log(b(b+1))}\\\\\n&amp;\\times\n(\\log y)^{-2+\\sum_{j=1}^{b-1}a_j\\nu_j+E_b}, \\qquad(4.4)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nE_b=\n2\\sum_{j=1}^{b-1}(\\nu_j-\\mu_j)\n+\\delta\\sum_{i=2}^b(i\\log i+i). \\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nEmpty sums are zero; when \\(b=1\\), \\(Y_1=Y_b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(0\\le i\\le b\\) and \\(0\\le j&lt;b\\), let\n\\[\ns_{i,j}=\\prod_{\\substack{p^a\\parallel p_j-1\\\\p\\le Y_i}}p^a,\n\\qquad\ns'_{i,j}=\\prod_{\\substack{p^a\\parallel q_j-1\\\\p\\le Y_i}}p^a.\n\\]\nRestricting the product equality to primes at most \\(Y_i\\) gives\n\\[\ns_i^\\#=D\\prod_{j=0}^{b-1}s_{i,j}\n      =D'\\prod_{j=0}^{b-1}s'_{i,j}.\n\\]\nFor \\(1\\le i\\le b\\), put\n\\[\nt_{i,j}=s_{i-1,j}/s_{i,j},\\qquad\nt'_{i,j}=s'_{i-1,j}/s'_{i,j},\\qquad\nt_i^\\#=s_{i-1}^\\#/s_i^\\#.\n\\]\nEach \\(t_i^\\#\\) is squarefree and supported on \\((Y_i,Y_{i-1}]\\). For \\(j\\ge i\\), both allocation factors equal \\(1\\), and\n\\[\nt_i^\\#=\\prod_{j=0}^{i-1}t_{i,j}\n      =\\prod_{j=0}^{i-1}t'_{i,j}. \\tag{4.6}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal C_i\\) be the configurations\n\\[\n\\sigma_i=(D',s_{i,0},\\ldots,s_{i,b-1},\n                 s'_{i,0},\\ldots,s'_{i,b-1})\n\\]\narising from genuine solutions. Restriction maps \\(\\mathcal C_{i-1}\\) into \\(\\mathcal C_i\\), and the two allocations in (4.6) determine an extension uniquely. The original tuples are in bijection with \\(\\mathcal C_0\\). For \\(i\\ge2\\), the appropriate extension weight is \\(1/t_i^\\#\\), because\n\\[\n1/s_{i-1}^\\#=1/(s_i^\\#t_i^\\#).\n\\]\nEvery configuration extends to a genuine solution, so its partial common product is at most \\(y/r\\), each shift is at most \\(y\\), and every fixed or conditional sieve coefficient is at most \\(y\\). Retain restrictions on conditional coefficients until the sieve has been applied.</p><p>Only the zeroth shift can have prime factors above \\(Y_1\\). Thus \\(t_1^\\#\\) is the same part of \\(p_0-1\\) and \\(q_0-1\\), and \\(t_1^\\#&gt;\\sqrt y\\). Normality gives\n\\[\n\\Omega(p_0-1)\n\\le B_y+\\log_2S+\\sqrt{B_y\\log_2S}\\le3B_y.\n\\]\nWrite \\(t_1^\\#=t'Q\\), where \\(Q=P^+(t_1^\\#)\\). The weaker bound \\(\\Omega(t_1^\\#)\\le4B_y\\) gives\n\\[\nQ\\ge y^{1/(8B_y)}.\n\\]\nAfter \\(\\sigma_1,t'\\) are fixed, the completed primes are \\(AQ+1,A'Q+1\\), with \\(A\\ne A'\\). Put\n\\[\nX=\\frac{y}{rs_1^\\#t'}.\n\\]\nA nonempty extension has \\(X\\ge Q\\ge y^{1/(8B_y)}\\). Lemma 7.1 bounds the number of \\(Q\\) by\n\\[\n\\ll\\frac{XB_y^3}{(\\log X)^3}\n\\ll\\frac{y}{rs_1^\\#t'}\\frac{B_y^6}{(\\log y)^3}.\n\\]\nSince \\(t'\\) is squarefree and supported on \\((Y_1,y]\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{t'}\\frac1{t'}\n\\le\\prod_{Y_1&lt;p\\le y}(1+1/p)\n\\ll\\frac{\\log y}{\\log Y_1}.\n\\]\nTherefore, for each \\(\\sigma_1\\),\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\sigma_0\\text{ restricting to }\\sigma_1\\}\n\\le\nCB_y^6\\frac{y}{rs_1^\\#}\n(\\log y)^{-2-\\nu_1}. \\tag{4.7}\n\\]</p><p>Now fix \\(2\\le i\\le b\\) and \\(\\sigma_i\\). The last slot, indexed \\(i-1\\), becomes complete in this layer. Let \\(Q_1,Q_2\\) be its largest prime factors on the two sides; both lie in \\([U_{i-1},Y_{i-1}]\\).</p><p>If \\(Q_1=Q_2=Q\\), remove its unique occurrence from \\(t_i^\\#\\), obtaining \\(t\\). For fixed allocations of \\(t\\), the completed primes are \\(AQ+1,A'Q+1\\), with \\(A\\ne A'\\). Lemma 7.1 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_Q\\frac1Q\\ll\\frac{B_y^3}{(\\log U_{i-1})^2}. \\tag{4.8}\n\\]</p><p>If \\(Q_1\\ne Q_2\\), remove both, obtaining \\(t\\). In addition to its allocations, fix the position of \\(Q_1\\) on the second side and of \\(Q_2\\) on the first; there are at most \\(i^2\\) choices. If neither lies in the opposite last slot, two applications of the two-form sieve give\n\\[\n\\sum_{Q_1,Q_2}\\frac1{Q_1Q_2}\n\\ll\\frac{B_y^4}{(\\log U_{i-1})^2}. \\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nIf \\(Q_2\\) lies in the first-side last slot, then \\(Q_2&lt;Q_1\\), and \\(Q_1\\) cannot lie in the second-side last slot. The conditions become\n\\[\nAQ_2Q_1+1,\\qquad A'Q_2+1.\n\\]\nFirst sum \\(Q_1\\) conditional on \\(Q_2\\). Feasibility gives \\(AQ_2\\le y\\), so the inner reciprocal sum is uniformly \\(O(B_y^2/\\log U_{i-1})\\). Only then enlarge the \\(Q_2\\)-range and apply the two-form sieve to \\(Q_2,A'Q_2+1\\). This proves (4.9). Reverse the summation order for the reversed incidence. Both incidences cannot occur, since they would imply both \\(Q_1&lt;Q_2\\) and \\(Q_2&lt;Q_1\\).</p><p>Put \\(\\Delta_i=\\nu_{i-1}-\\nu_i\\). Normality bounds the number of prime factors of an individual shift in this layer by\n\\[\n(\\Delta_i+\\delta)B_y.\n\\]\nIf the shift ends before \\(Y_{i-1}\\), apply normality only to its endpoint; if it ends before \\(Y_i\\), the count is zero. In either case the error is at most \\(\\delta B_y\\). Only \\(i\\) shifts contribute, so\n\\[\n\\Omega(t)\\le I_i:=i(\\Delta_i+\\delta)B_y.\n\\]\nSquarefreeness gives at most \\(i^{2\\Omega(t)}\\) dual allocations. With\n\\[\nM_i=\\sum_{Y_i&lt;p\\le Y_{i-1}}\\frac1p,\n\\]\nMertens’ estimate and \\(\\delta B_y\\ge\\sqrt{B_y}\\) give\n\\[\nM_i\\le(\\Delta_i+\\delta)B_y\n\\]\nafter increasing \\(y_0\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\sum_t\\frac{i^{2\\Omega(t)}}t\n\\le\\sum_{n\\le I_i}\\frac{(i^2M_i)^n}{n!}\n\\le i^{I_i}e^{iM_i}\n\\le\n\\exp\\{(i\\log i+i)(\\Delta_i+\\delta)B_y\\}. \\tag{4.10}\n\\]\nThe middle inequality writes \\(i^{2n}=i^ni^n\\), uses \\(i^n\\le i^{I_i}\\), and extends the remaining exponential series.</p><p>Combining (4.8)–(4.10), and absorbing position choices using \\(i\\le b\\le B_y\\), gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\sigma_{i-1}\\text{ restricting to }\\sigma_i}\\frac1{t_i^\\#}\n\\le\nCB_y^6\n(\\log y)^{-2\\mu_{i-1}+(i\\log i+i)(\\nu_{i-1}-\\nu_i+\\delta)}.\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]\nSquarefreeness was used both in removing one occurrence of each distinguished prime and in obtaining the factorial denominator.</p><p>Starting from (4.7), sum successively using\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\sigma_{i-1}\\in\\mathcal C_{i-1}}\\frac1{s_{i-1}^\\#}\n=\n\\sum_{\\sigma_i\\in\\mathcal C_i}\\frac1{s_i^\\#}\n\\sum_{\\sigma_{i-1}\\text{ restricting to }\\sigma_i}\\frac1{t_i^\\#}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(c_i=i\\log i+i\\), so \\(c_1=1\\). The accumulated exponent of \\(\\log y\\) is\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2-\\nu_1\n+\\sum_{i=2}^b\\{-2\\mu_{i-1}+c_i(\\nu_{i-1}-\\nu_i+\\delta)\\}\n={}&amp;-2+\\sum_{j=1}^{b-1}a_j\\nu_j\\\\\n&amp;+2\\sum_{j=1}^{b-1}(\\nu_j-\\mu_j)\n+\\delta\\sum_{i=2}^bc_i-c_b\\nu_b.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(c_{j+1}-c_j-2=a_j\\), including \\(j=1\\). The identity also holds for \\(b=1\\). Discard the nonpositive final term.</p><p>It remains to sum \\(1/s_b^\\#\\). Write \\(s=s_b^\\#/D\\), \\(L_b=\\log_2Y_b\\). A fixed \\(s\\) has at most \\(b^{\\Omega(s)}\\) allocations to left slots and at most \\((b+1)^{\\Omega(sD)}\\) allocations to right slots and \\(D'\\). These bounds allow prime powers by assigning labeled prime occurrences.</p><p>Normality gives, for every left shift,\n\\[\n\\Omega(p_j-1,1,Y_b)\n\\le2\\log_2S+(L_b-\\log_2S)+\\sqrt{L_b\\log_2S}\n\\le3L_b.\n\\]\nIf \\(S=Y_b\\), the first normality condition suffices. Hence \\(\\Omega(s)\\le3bL_b\\), and\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{\\sigma_b\\in\\mathcal C_b}\\frac1{s_b^\\#}\n&amp;\\le\n\\frac{(b+1)^{\\Omega(D)}}D\n[b(b+1)]^{3bL_b}\n\\sum_{P^+(s)\\le Y_b}\\frac1s\\\\\n&amp;\\ll\n\\frac{(b+1)^{\\Omega(D)}}D\n(\\log Y_b)^{1+3b\\log(b(b+1))}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nCombining these estimates proves (4.4).</p><p>As recorded in original Remark 4.3,\n\\[\nE_b=\n2\\sum_{j=1}^{b-1}(\\nu_j-\\mu_j)\n+O(\\delta b^2\\log(2b)),\n\\]\nand the smooth-factor exponent is \\(O(b\\log(2b))\\). No restriction such as \\(r\\le y^{1/10}\\) or \\(D\\le y^{1/100}\\) is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Conditions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions.\nLet \\(y\\ge y_0\\), \\(B_y=\\log_2y\\), and let \\(1\\le A,A'\\le y\\) be integers. Consider either\n\\[\nn,\\ An+1\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nn,\\ An+1,\\ A'n+1,\\qquad A\\ne A'.\n\\]\nLet \\(k=2\\) or \\(3\\), respectively, and let \\(\\mathcal P\\) be the positive integer inputs at which all indicated forms are prime.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With absolute constants,\n\\[\n\\#(\\mathcal P\\cap[1,X])\n\\le C\\frac{XB_y^k}{(\\log X)^k}\n\\quad(X\\ge3), \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\in\\mathcal P\\\\U\\le n\\le Y}}\\frac1n\n\\le C\\frac{B_y^k}{(\\log U)^{k-1}}\n\\quad(3\\le U\\le Y). \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nThe coefficients need not be bounded in terms of \\(X\\) or \\(U\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For primitive, distinct, admissible integral linear forms \\(a_jn+b_j\\), with positive leading coefficients and nonzero determinant, Ford’s uniform upper sieve [8, Theorem 2.5] gives\n\\[\n\\#\\{1\\le n\\le X:a_jn+b_j\\text{ prime for every }j\\}\n\\ll_k\n\\frac X{(\\log X)^k}\n\\left(\\frac{\\Delta}{\\varphi(\\Delta)}\\right)^k,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Delta=\n\\left|\\prod_ja_j\\prod_{j&lt;l}(a_jb_l-a_lb_j)\\right|. \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nThe implied constant depends only on \\(k\\), including when coefficients vary. The present determinants are \\(A\\) and \\(AA'(A-A')\\), up to sign, so they are nonzero and at most \\(y^3\\) in absolute value.</p><p>If the forms are inadmissible, an offending prime is at most \\(k\\), since each primitive form has at most one root modulo that prime. At every simultaneous-prime input, one form must equal that prime. There are at most \\(k\\) such inputs, absorbed in (4.1).</p><p>For admissible forms,\n\\[\n\\frac d{\\varphi(d)}\\ll\\log_2(d+10).\n\\]\nTo recall uniformity, split prime divisors at \\(\\log(d+10)\\). Mertens’ product estimate bounds the first part by \\(O(\\log_2(d+10))\\); for the remainder use \\(\\sum_{p\\mid d}\\log p\\le\\log d\\) to obtain a bounded factor. Equation (4.3) proves (4.1).</p><p>Partial summation, with coefficients fixed, gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{n\\in\\mathcal P\\\\U\\le n\\le Y}}\\frac1n\n\\ll\nB_y^k\\left\\{\n\\frac1{(\\log Y)^k}\n+\\int_U^Y\\frac{dt}{t(\\log t)^k}\n\\right\\}\n\\ll\\frac{B_y^k}{(\\log U)^{k-1}}.\n\\]\nThis includes a possible prime at the lower endpoint.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 5.1, “Prefix collisions.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions and definitions.\nUse the tuple sets \\(\\mathcal T(t),\\mathcal G(t),\\mathcal D(t)\\) of Proposition 6, with all parameters formed from \\(x\\), \\(H\\) fixed before \\(x\\to\\infty\\), and \\(t=x\\) or \\(t=x/c\\) for fixed \\(c&gt;1\\). Define\n\\[\nF(p_0,\\ldots,p_R,d)=d\\prod_{j=0}^R(p_j-1),\n\\]\n\\[\nK(t)=\n\\#\\{(\\tau,\\sigma)\\in\\mathcal G(t)^2:\n\\tau\\ne\\sigma,\\ F(\\tau)=F(\\sigma)\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a phase-independent \\(\\epsilon_H\\to0\\) such that\n\\[\nK(t)\\le\n\\{\\epsilon_H+o_{x;H}(1)\\}\\frac{xG_m}{\\log x}.\n\\]\nThe pairs are ordered pairs of distinct tuples; tail preimages of \\(d\\) are not part of a tuple.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose one basic witness for each tuple. Every choice satisfies (3.18). Distinct tuples with the same value differ at some displayed prime. Let \\(i\\) be the first unequal index, write \\(q_j\\) for the other witness’s primes, and put\n\\[\nh=m-i,\\quad J=\\lceil30\\log h\\rceil,\\quad\nz=\\exp\\exp(0.7b_{i+J}),\\quad\nS=\\exp\\exp(b_i^{1/3}).\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nD_i=\\varphi(p_{i+J+1}\\cdots p_La),\\qquad\nD'_i=\\varphi(q_{i+J+1}\\cdots q_La').\n\\]\nAfter canceling the common prefix,\n\\[\nv^{(i)}\n=\nD_i\\prod_{j=0}^J(p_{i+j}-1)\n=\nD'_i\\prod_{j=0}^J(q_{i+j}-1). \\tag{5.1}\n\\]</p><p>Uniformly for \\(h\\ge H\\),\n\\[\nb_i\\asymp h\\rho^{-h},\\qquad\n\\log b_i=O(h),\\qquad\nc_1h^{-20}\\le\n\\frac{b_{i+J}}{b_i}\n=(1-J/h)\\rho^J\n\\le C_1h^{-18}. \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nIn particular \\(J&lt;h/2\\) and \\(i+J&lt;L\\).</p><p>For \\(i=0\\), set \\(y=x\\). For \\(i\\ge1\\), place \\(v^{(i)}\\) in a dyadic interval \\((y/2,y]\\). The logarithm of the preimage suffix after \\(p_i\\) is at most\n\\[\nh\\exp(0.62b_i)+\\exp(2\\widetilde b_L),\n\\]\nwhich is negligible compared with \\(\\log p_i\\ge\\exp(0.88b_i)\\). Consequently,\n\\[\np_i,q_i\\ge y^{0.9},\\qquad\n0.88b_i\\le B_y:=\\log_2y\\le1.12b_i,\\qquad\nu_i\\le B_y+O(1). \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nFor \\(i=0\\), \\(B_y=b_0=u_0=B\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\delta=\\sqrt{\\log_2S/B_y}\\asymp b_i^{-1/3}.\n\\]\nThen \\(S&lt;z\\) and \\(J\\delta=o(h^{-20})\\). Normality gives\n\\[\n\\Omega(p_{i+j}-1),\\ \\Omega(q_{i+j}-1)\\le4B_y\n\\quad(0\\le j\\le J).\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\nA_j=P^+(p_{i+j}-1),\\qquad\nA'_j=P^+(q_{i+j}-1),\n\\]\nthe inequalities\n\\[\np_{i+j}-1\\le A_j^{\\Omega(p_{i+j}-1)},\n\\qquad A_j\\le p_{i+j}-1\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\n\\log_2A_j=u_{i+j}+O(\\log b_i),\\qquad\n\\log_2A'_j=\\log_2q_{i+j}+O(\\log b_i). \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nWhen \\(i=j=0\\), \\(u_0=B\\) differs from \\(\\log_2p_0\\) by \\(O(1)\\).</p><p>These bands are disjoint and decreasing; their smallest separation is a constant multiple of \\(b_ih^{-20}\\), which dominates \\(O(\\log b_i)\\). All \\(A_j,A'_j\\) exceed \\(z\\), while \\(D_i,D'_i\\) are \\(z\\)-smooth.</p><p>We claim\n\\[\n|\\log_2A_j-\\log_2A'_j|\n\\le(2j+1)\\delta B_y\n\\quad(0\\le j\\le J). \\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nSuppose \\(A_j&gt;A'_j\\), and put\n\\[\ne=(\\log_2A_j-\\log_2A'_j)/B_y.\n\\]\nOn the first side of (5.1), each of the first \\(j+1\\) shifts contributes at least \\((e-\\delta)B_y\\) prime factors to \\((A'_j,A_j]\\). On the other side only the first \\(j\\) shifts contribute, each at most \\((e+\\delta)B_y\\). The lower endpoint exceeds \\(S\\), so normality applies. Equality gives\n\\[\n(j+1)(e-\\delta)\\le j(e+\\delta),\n\\]\nproving the claim. Interchange sides for the other order.</p><p>Cancel any equal primes in the two lists, and let \\(r\\) be the product of their shifts. Equality is possible only at the same original index. Index \\(i\\) survives. Let \\(b\\le J+1\\le B_y\\) be the remaining length. Corresponding remaining primes are unequal.</p><p>Set \\(Y_b=z\\), \\(Y_0=y\\), and \\(\\log_2U_0=0.8B_y\\). At each remaining positive index, choose an interval on a \\(\\delta\\)-grid in normalized double logarithms containing the pair in (5.5). Its width is \\(O(J\\delta)\\). The intervals remain separated, lie below height \\(0.8\\), and the zeroth largest factors lie above height \\(0.9\\). Thus the ordered-cutoff hypotheses of Proposition 7 hold.</p><p>The part of \\(p_i-1\\) supported at most \\(Y_1\\) has logarithm at most\n\\[\n4B_y\\log Y_1\\le4B_y\\exp(0.8B_y).\n\\]\nSince \\(p_i\\ge y^{0.9}\\), the complementary part exceeds \\(\\sqrt y\\). The common product is squarefree above \\(z\\), and cancellation preserves this. Apply Proposition 7 with \\(D=D_i,D'=D'_i\\) and canceled factor \\(r\\).</p><p>Let \\(j_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;j_{b-1}\\) be the surviving positive original indices. Since \\(j_k\\ge k\\) and \\(a_k\\le a_{j_k}\\), the grid intervals and strict slack give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{k=1}^{b-1}a_k\\nu_k\n&amp;\\le\\frac1{B_y}\\sum_{j=1}^Ja_ju_{i+j}\n+O(J^2\\log(2J)\\delta)\\\\\n&amp;\\le1-h^{-4}\n+O(B_y^{-1}+J^2\\log(2J)\\delta). \\qquad(5.6)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe grid widths and explicit \\(\\delta\\)-term contribute another \\(o(h^{-4})\\) to the exponent. Hence the logarithmic factor in (4.4) is at most\n\\[\n(\\log y)^{-1-\\kappa h^{-4}}\n=\n\\frac1{\\log y}\\exp(-\\kappa B_y/h^4)\n\\]\nfor an absolute \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\).</p><p>Recover the tuple data hidden in \\(D_i,D'_i\\). If \\(R\\le i+J\\), the tuple primes are determined and\n\\[\nd=D_i\\prod_{j=R+1}^{i+J}(p_j-1).\n\\]\nIf \\(R&gt;i+J\\), the missing data give an ordered factorization\n\\[\nD_i=d\\prod_{j=i+J+1}^R(p_j-1). \\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nAssigning prime occurrences to at most \\(h+1\\) labeled slots bounds their number by \\((h+1)^{\\Omega(D_i)}\\). The two tails cost at most\n\\[\n(h+1)^{\\Omega(D_i)+\\Omega(D'_i)}\n\\le\\exp\\{2b_i\\log(h+1)/h^8\\}. \\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nThe factorizations are exact because the displayed primes are distinct and the preimage tail is smaller than \\(p_R\\).</p><p>Sum over canceled index sets, canceled primes, grids, and \\(D_i\\). The index sets cost \\(2^{J+1}\\); each canceled prime has reciprocal shift sum \\(O(b_i)\\); grid choices are at most \\((C/\\delta)^{2(J+1)}\\). Including \\((CB_y^6)^b\\), these cost \\(\\exp(O(h^2))\\). Also,\n\\[\n\\sum_{P^+(D)\\le z}\\frac1D\\ll\\log z.\n\\]\nBefore enlarging this sum, use\n\\[\n\\Omega(D_i),\\Omega(D'_i)\\le b_i/h^8\n\\]\nin (5.8) and in \\((b+1)^{\\Omega(D_i)}\\). The logarithm of all remaining costs is at most\n\\[\nO(h^2)\n+O\\left(\\frac{b_i\\log h}{h^8}\\right)\n+O\\left(\\frac{b_i\\log h\\log\\log(3h)}{h^{18}}\\right)\n=o(b_i/h^4). \\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\log_2z\\ll b_ih^{-18}\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{ordered suffix tuple pairs}:y/2&lt;v^{(i)}\\le y\\}\n\\ll\n\\frac y{\\log y}\\exp(-c_2B_y/h^4). \\tag{5.10}\n\\]\nFor \\(i=0\\), the same expression bounds all pairs with first unequal index zero.</p><p>For \\(i\\ge1\\), sum suffix pairs with weight \\(1/v^{(i)}\\). A dyadic interval contributes at most\n\\[\n\\frac C{\\log y}\\exp(-c_2\\log_2y/h^4).\n\\]\nWriting \\(y=2^n\\), comparison with an integral gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{\\text{suffix pairs}}\\frac1{v^{(i)}}\n&amp;\\ll\n\\sum_{\\log(n\\log2)\\ge0.88b_i-O(1)}\n\\frac{\\exp\\{-c_2\\log(n\\log2)/h^4\\}}{n\\log2}\\\\\n&amp;\\ll\n\\int_{0.88b_i-O(1)}^\\infty e^{-c_2u/h^4}\\,du\n\\ll h^4e^{-c_3b_i/h^4}. \\qquad(5.11)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nNo factor of order \\(\\exp(b_i)\\) is lost.</p><p>For fixed suffix data and common \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_{i-1}\\), the number of \\(p_0\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{Cx}{\\log x}\n\\frac1{v^{(i)}\\prod_{j=1}^{i-1}(p_j-1)}.\n\\]\nIf the range is nonempty, its endpoint is at least \\(x^{0.9}\\), so the prime number theorem applies with logarithm comparable to \\(\\log x\\). Proposition 3 restores the common prefix. The contribution for \\(i\\) is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{CxG_m}{\\log x}h^4e^{-c_3b_i/h^4}.\n\\]\nSum over \\(h\\ge H\\):\n\\[\nC\\sum_{h\\ge H}h^4\\exp\\{-c_4\\rho^{-h}/h^3\\}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nFor \\(i=0\\), (5.10) gives\n\\[\nO\\left(\\frac x{\\log x}e^{-c_2B/m^4}\\right)\n=o(xG_m/\\log x).\n\\]\nThe comparison data and recovered factorizations determine each ordered tuple pair, so choosing witnesses has introduced no multiplicity into the desired count.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 5.3, “A common prefix for all preimages.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions.\nUse \\(\\mathcal T(t),\\mathcal G(t),\\mathcal D(t)\\) and \\(F\\) from Propositions 6 and 8. All parameters are formed from \\(x\\), and \\(t=x\\) or \\(t=x/c\\), with fixed \\(c&gt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(\\epsilon_H\\to0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\bigl||\\mathcal T(t)|-V(t)\\bigr|\n\\le\n\\{\\epsilon_H+o_{x;H}(1)\\}\\frac{xG_m}{\\log x}. \\tag{5.12}\n\\]\nThere is a set \\(\\mathcal E^*(t)\\subseteq\\mathcal V\\cap[1,t]\\) for which both its cardinality and the number of tuples above it are at most the same bound, after enlarging \\(\\epsilon_H\\).</p><p>For every \\(v\\in(\\mathcal V\\cap[1,t])\\setminus\\mathcal E^*(t)\\), there is a unique tuple \\((p_0,\\ldots,p_R,d)\\in\\mathcal T(t)\\) of value \\(v\\). Every preimage of \\(v\\) has this prefix and a remaining factor of totient \\(d\\). In particular,\n\\[\n\\ell(v)=p_0\\cdots p_R\\ell(d). \\tag{5.13}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 9.1 with\n\\[\nT=\\mathcal T(t),\\quad G=\\mathcal G(t),\\quad\nD=\\mathcal D(t),\\quad W=\\mathcal V\\cap[1,t],\n\\]\nand let \\(E\\) be the exceptional set from Proposition 5 restricted to \\([1,t]\\). Every preimage of \\(v\\notin E\\) is basic and induces a tuple. Proposition 1 converts the exceptional-value bound to the scale \\(xG_m/\\log x\\); Proposition 6 bounds discarded tuples, and Proposition 8 bounds \\(K\\). The counting conclusions follow.</p><p>Fix \\(v\\notin\\mathcal E^*(t)\\). Every preimage induces a tuple; none is discarded, and the remaining tuple is unique. Thus, with \\(Q=p_0\\cdots p_R\\), every preimage is \\(Qz\\) with \\(\\varphi(z)=d\\), and is at least \\(Q\\ell(d)\\).</p><p>Choose one basic witness with tail \\(w\\). Proposition 5 gives\n\\[\n\\ell(d)\\le w&lt;p_R.\n\\]\nThe prefix primes are distinct, so \\(\\gcd(Q,\\ell(d))=1\\), and\n\\[\n\\varphi(Q\\ell(d))\n=\n\\varphi(Q)\\varphi(\\ell(d))\n=\nd\\prod_{j=0}^R(p_j-1)=v.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\ell(v)\\le Q\\ell(d)\\), proving (5.13). The universal quantifier over preimages in Proposition 5 is needed for the reverse inequality; a single basic preimage would give only the upper bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, “Finite-map counting.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Conditions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions and definitions.\nLet \\(T,W\\) be finite sets, \\(F:T\\to W\\), and \\(T=G\\sqcup D\\). Suppose every element of \\(W\\setminus E\\) lies in \\(F(T)\\), where \\(E\\subseteq W\\). Put\n\\[\nr_w=|G\\cap F^{-1}(w)|,\\qquad\nK=\\sum_{w\\in W}r_w(r_w-1),\n\\]\n\\[\nE^*=E\\cup F(D)\\cup\\{w:r_w\\ge2\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\n-|E|\\le|T|-|W|\\le|D|+K/2,\n\\]\n\\[\n|E^*|\\le|E|+|D|+K/2,\\qquad\n|F^{-1}(E^*)|\\le|E|+2|D|+K.\n\\]\nThe restriction of \\(F\\) to \\(T\\setminus F^{-1}(E^*)\\) is a bijection onto \\(W\\setminus E^*\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Coverage gives the lower bound. For every positive integer \\(r\\),\n\\[\nr-1\\le r(r-1)/2,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n|G|-|F(G)|\\le K/2.\n\\]\nAdding at most \\(|D|\\) remaining elements proves the upper bound. There are at most \\(K/2\\) fibers with \\(r_w\\ge2\\), giving the bound for \\(|E^*|\\). Also\n\\[\n\\sum_{w\\in E^*}r_w\\le|E^*|+K/2,\n\\]\nby \\(r\\le1+r(r-1)/2\\) for integers \\(r\\ge0\\). Adding the elements of \\(D\\) proves the second bound. Outside \\(E^*\\), coverage, absence of discarded elements, and \\(r_w\\le1\\) give exactly one preimage.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 6.1, “The unweighted prime sum.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions and definitions.\nUse the basic-witness setting of Proposition 5, with \\(H\\) fixed first, \\(x\\to\\infty\\) next, and \\(H\\to\\infty\\) last.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal D_H(x)\\) be the distinct data \\((d,p_1,\\ldots,p_R)\\) admitting primes \\(p_{R+1},\\ldots,p_L\\) and an integer \\(a\\ge1\\) satisfying (3.10) with \\(p_0\\) omitted, such that\n\\[\nw=p_{R+1}\\cdots p_La,\\qquad d=\\varphi(w).\n\\]\nThe convention \\(u_0=B\\) is retained, so the index-zero simplex inequality is independent of the eventual \\(p_0\\).</p><p>For \\(f:[1,\\infty)\\to[0,1]\\), define\n\\[\nM_f(x;H)=\n\\sum_{(d,p_1,\\ldots,p_R)\\in\\mathcal D_H(x)}\n\\frac{f(\\ell(d)/d)}{d\\prod_{i=1}^R(p_i-1)}. \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nEach datum is counted once, regardless of its witnesses.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\n0\\le M_f(x;H)\\le M_1(x;H)\\ll K_HG_m. \\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nFor each fixed \\(c&gt;1\\), both \\(t=x\\) and \\(t=x/c\\), with parameters formed from the same \\(x\\), satisfy\n\\[\nV(t)=\\frac t{\\log x}\n\\bigl(M_1(x;H)+E_H(t;x)G_m\\bigr), \\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\lim_{H\\to\\infty}\\limsup_{x\\to\\infty}\n\\max_{t\\in\\{x,x/c\\}}|E_H(t;x)|=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Supermultiplicativity gives\n\\[\n\\frac1{\\varphi(p_{R+1}\\cdots p_La)}\n\\le\n\\frac1{\\varphi(a)}\n\\prod_{i=R+1}^L\\frac1{p_i-1}. \\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nSumming over witnesses, Proposition 3 bounds the full prime-coordinate reciprocal sum by \\(O(G_m)\\), and Proposition 4 bounds the smooth cofactor sum by \\(O(K_H)\\). This proves (6.3), including the case \\(p_L\\mid a\\).</p><p>For fixed data put\n\\[\nD=d\\prod_{i=1}^R(p_i-1).\n\\]\nBy Proposition 5,\n\\[\n1\\le D\\le p_1\\cdots p_La\\le\\exp((\\log x)^{0.8}).\n\\]\nThe possible largest primes are precisely\n\\[\nx^{0.9}\\le p_0\\le1+t/D.\n\\]\nAll other preimage prime factors are smaller than \\(x^{0.9}\\), so no repetition or ordering restriction is introduced. Uniformly,\n\\[\nt/D\\in[x^{1-o(1)}/c,x],\\qquad\n\\log(t/D)=\\log x+o(\\log x).\n\\]\nThe prime number theorem gives\n\\[\n\\#\\{p_0:x^{0.9}\\le p_0\\le1+t/D\\}\n=\n\\frac t{D\\log x}\n\\bigl(1+o_{x\\to\\infty;H,c}(1)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe lower cutoff costs a relative \\(O(x^{-0.1+o(1)})\\). Sum over the data using (6.3), then apply Proposition 9 to replace tuple counts by \\(V(t)\\). Since \\(t=x\\) or \\(x/c\\), the resulting normalized iterated error is zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Corollary 6.2, “Fixed scaling.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions.\nLet \\(V(x)\\) count distinct totients, as in Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed real \\(c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{V(cx)}{V(x)}\\longrightarrow c.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(c&gt;1\\), apply Proposition 10 at \\(t=x\\) and \\(t=x/c\\), with the same \\(M_1(x;H)\\) and \\(G_m(x)\\). Subtraction gives\n\\[\n\\frac{(V(x)-cV(x/c))\\log x}{xG_m}\n=\nE_H(x;x)-E_H(x/c;x).\n\\]\nProposition 1 supplies \\(c_-&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\nV(x)\\log x/(xG_m)\\ge c_-\n\\]\nfor large \\(x\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\limsup_{x\\to\\infty}\n\\left|1-\\frac{cV(x/c)}{V(x)}\\right|\n\\le\n\\frac1{c_-}\n\\limsup_{x\\to\\infty}\n\\bigl(|E_H(x;x)|+|E_H(x/c;x)|\\bigr).\n\\]\nLet \\(H\\to\\infty\\), then replace \\(x\\) by \\(cx\\). This also covers points where \\(m(x)\\ne m(x/c)\\), since the latter integer never enters the comparison. No continuity or endpoint matching of the phase coefficient is needed.</p><p>The case \\(c=1\\) is immediate. For \\(0&lt;c&lt;1\\), apply the proved result to \\(1/c\\) at \\(y=cx\\), and take reciprocals.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma 6.4, “The volume of the witness union.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions and definitions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions and definitions.\nUse \\(M_f\\) from Proposition 10 and the arithmetic witnesses from Theorem 1. Fix \\(H\\), and put \\(s=\\theta(x)\\), \\(R=m-H\\). For \\(\\eta\\in\\mathcal W_{H,s}(d)\\), let \\(\\mathcal K_\\eta\\subseteq\\mathbb R^R\\) be defined by \\(u_0=B\\) and\n\\[\nu_i-\\sum_{r=i+1}^Ra_{r-i}u_r\n\\ge D(m-i,\\eta)\n\\quad(0\\le i\\le R). \\tag{6.6}\n\\]\nThese inequalities imply \\(u_i\\ge0\\). The coordinates are real, and the coarse prefix bands have been removed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(\\delta_H\\to0\\), independent of \\(f:[1,\\infty)\\to[0,1]\\), such that\n\\[\n\\limsup_{x\\to\\infty}\n\\left|\n\\frac{M_f(x;H)}{G_m}\n-\n\\frac1{G_m}\n\\sum_{d:\\mathcal W_{H,\\theta}(d)\\ne\\varnothing}\n\\frac{f(\\ell(d)/d)}d\n\\operatorname{Vol}_R\n\\bigcup_{\\eta\\in\\mathcal W_{H,\\theta}(d)}\\mathcal K_\\eta\n\\right|\n\\le\\delta_H. \\tag{6.7}\n\\]\nFor every nonempty \\(T\\subseteq\\mathcal W_{H,\\theta}(d)\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Vol}_R\\bigcap_{\\eta\\in T}\\mathcal K_\\eta\n=\nG_R\n\\left(\n1-\\frac1B\\sum_{h=H}^mg_{m-h}\n\\max_{\\eta\\in T}D(h,\\eta)\n\\right)_+^R. \\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nAll estimates are uniform in phase.</p>", "proof": "<p>For an intersection, put\n\\[\nC_i=\\max_{\\eta\\in T}D(m-i,\\eta),\n\\qquad\nt_i=u_i-\\sum_{r=i+1}^Ra_{r-i}u_r.\n\\]\nProposition 1 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^Rg_it_i=B,\n\\]\nand the substitution from positive-index coordinates to slacks has determinant \\(1\\). The intersection is \\(t_i\\ge C_i\\), including \\(i=0\\). Translation leaves a simplex with available size \\(B-\\sum_i g_iC_i\\), hence volume\n\\[\n\\frac{(B-\\sum_{i=0}^Rg_iC_i)_+^R}\n{R!\\prod_{i=1}^Rg_i}.\n\\]\nThis proves (6.8).</p><p>Identify \\(Q_h=p_{m-h}\\) for \\(P\\le h&lt;H\\). For fixed tail witness, the original prefix conditions are its coarse bands and\n\\[\n\\xi_iu_i-\\sum_{r=i+1}^Ra_{r-i}u_r\n\\ge D(m-i,\\eta)\n\\quad(0\\le i\\le R). \\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nTaking the union over witnesses of a fixed \\(d\\) is essential, because each prefix datum contributes once.</p><p>First replace reciprocal prime sums by the volume of this union, retaining bands and \\(\\xi_i\\). For a unit box wholly contained in the union, (3.12) gives relative error\n\\[\nO\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^Re^{-cb_i}\\right)\n=\nO(e^{-c'b_R}).\n\\]\nThe geometric growth of \\(b_i\\) backwards makes this independent of \\(R\\). Total mass and total volume are \\(O(K_HG_m)\\), by summing over witnesses using (6.2).</p><p>If a box meets the union but is not contained in it, choose a witness region meeting it. That region does not contain the box, so the box lies at a prefix band endpoint or within \\(O(h_i^2)\\) of equality in (6.9). For an upper bound sum these shells over witnesses using (6.2), thicken the discrete tail coordinates into unit boxes, and sum \\(1/\\varphi(a)\\) at cost \\(O(K_H)\\). Proposition 3 gives total cost at most\n\\[\nO\\left(\nK_H\\sum_{h\\ge H}(he^{-h/40}+h^3\\rho^h)\n\\right)G_m=o_H(1)G_m.\n\\]\nThe broader \\(O(K_HH^{-2})G_m\\) shell bound also suffices.</p><p>Next replace \\(\\xi_i\\) by \\(1\\), retaining bands. The removed region lies in a shell of thickness \\(O(e^{-h_i/40}b_i)\\), with the same thickening errors. Its normalized contribution is bounded by the preceding \\(o_H(1)\\) expression. These union bounds may sum over witnesses since \\(0\\le f\\le1\\).</p><p>It remains to remove prefix bands. Every point satisfying the unperturbed inequalities obeys, by recurrence iteration,\n\\[\nu_i\\ge g_{R+1-i}v_{H-1}\\gg H\\rho^{-h_i}\n\\quad(0\\le i\\le R), \\tag{6.10}\n\\]\nwhere \\(v_{H-1}=\\log_2Q_{H-1}\\). Retain the first tail coordinate’s contribution in each prefix inequality, discard other nonnegative tail contributions, and apply the recurrence for \\(g\\). Its band and (3.5) give the displayed lower bound.</p><p>Thickening the \\(H-P\\) tail coordinates changes inequality \\(i\\) by \\(O(H\\log(h_i+1))\\). The enlargement term\n\\[\nCh_i^2\\rho^{h_i}u_i\n\\]\nis at least a constant times \\(CHh_i^2\\), so absorbs this error. Together with tail bands, the full region lies in the enlarged simplex of Proposition 3.</p><p>To sum without a witness-count factor, use (6.2), fix \\(a\\), and partition tail coordinates into half-open unit boxes\n\\[\nC=\\prod_{h=P}^{H-1}C_h.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\mathcal U_C\\) be the union of \\(\\mathcal K_\\eta\\) for allowed witnesses with tail coordinates in \\(C\\), and let \\(E\\) be the prefix points violating a removed band. Then\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\eta=((Q_h),a)\\text{ allowed}\\\\(\\log_2Q_h)_h\\in C}}\n\\frac{\\operatorname{Vol}_R(\\mathcal K_\\eta\\cap E)}\n{\\prod_h(Q_h-1)}\n&amp;\\le\n\\left(\\prod_{h=P}^{H-1}\n\\sum_{\\log_2q\\in C_h}\\frac1{q-1}\\right)\n\\operatorname{Vol}_R(\\mathcal U_C\\cap E)\\\\\n&amp;\\ll\n\\operatorname{Vol}_L((\\mathcal U_C\\cap E)\\times C).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe sums over \\(q\\) are over primes. Their product is \\(O(1)\\), uniformly in the number of tail coordinates. Since \\(R+(H-P)=L\\), and the tail boxes are disjoint, the volumes sum to a subset of one enlarged \\(L\\)-simplex, counted once. Summing \\(1/\\varphi(a)\\) costs \\(O(K_H)\\).</p><p>Scale the enlarged simplex into the true \\(L\\)-simplex. The Jacobian cost is bounded, and the prefix coordinate factors are\n\\[\n1+O\\left(\\sum_{h\\ge H}(e^{-h/40}+h^2\\rho^h)\\right)\n=\n1+o_H(1).\n\\]\nFor large \\(H\\), then large \\(x\\), violation of a removed\n\\([0.9\\widetilde b_i,1.1\\widetilde b_i]\\) band becomes violation of\n\\([0.95b_i,1.05b_i]\\) in the true simplex. Proposition 2 applies with dimension \\(L=m-P\\), since \\(h_i\\ge H\\) and \\(P/H\\to0\\). Its summed probability is \\(O(e^{-cH})\\), so the added volume is\n\\[\nO(K_He^{-cH})G_m=o_H(1)G_m.\n\\]\nThis proves (6.7). Uniformity follows from\n\\[\n\\lambda\\le\\alpha_\\theta&lt;\\lambda/\\rho,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha/\\alpha_\\theta=1+o_{x\\to\\infty;H}(1).\n\\]\nThe estimates are made at each phase and do not compare witness sets at nearby phases.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.3, “Uniformly finite tail data.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Conditions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions.\nUse the tail witnesses \\(\\mathcal W_{H,s}(d)\\) and \\(D(h,\\eta)\\) defined in Theorem 1, for sufficiently large fixed \\(H\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The union of all \\(\\mathcal W_{H,s}(d)\\), over \\(0\\le s&lt;1\\) and all \\(d\\), is finite. Every prime coordinate, integer coordinate, and totient value has an explicit bound depending only on \\(H\\). Uniformly over these data,\n\\[\n0\\le D(h,\\eta)\\ll_H\\log(2h)\n\\quad(h\\ge H). \\tag{6.5}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(a_*=\\lambda/\\rho\\), so \\(\\lambda\\le\\alpha_s&lt;a_*\\), and put\n\\[\nq_H=\\left\\lceil\\exp\\exp(1.1a_*H\\rho^{-H})\\right\\rceil,\n\\qquad\nA_H^*=\\left\\lceil\\exp\\exp(2a_*P\\rho^{-P})\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nEvery witness belongs to\n\\[\n\\{1,\\ldots,q_H\\}^{H-P}\\times\\{1,\\ldots,A_H^*\\},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nd\\le w\\le A_H^*q_H^{H-P}.\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\na_j=\\int_j^{j+1}\\log t\\,dt\\le\\log(j+1),\n\\]\nso the bounded tail coordinates give (6.5).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 6.5, “Approximation by the finite coefficient.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions.\nUse \\(M_f(x;H)\\) from Proposition 10 and \\(A_H(f;s)\\) from Theorem 1, with \\(f:[1,\\infty)\\to[0,1]\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(\\varepsilon_H\\to0\\), independent of \\(f\\) and phase, such that\n\\[\n\\limsup_{x\\to\\infty}\n\\left|\n\\frac{M_f(x;H)}{G_m}-A_H(f;\\theta(x))\n\\right|\n\\le\\varepsilon_H. \\tag{6.11}\n\\]\nEach \\(A_H(f;s)\\) is a finite sum involving \\(f\\) at explicitly bounded rational arguments and absolutely convergent series in its exponential weights. Also \\(A_H(f;s)\\ge0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply finite inclusion–exclusion to the union in Proposition 12 and use (6.8). For a nonempty subset \\(T\\) of witnesses put\n\\[\nC_T(h)=\\max_{\\eta\\in T}D(h,\\eta),\\qquad\nS_T=\\sum_{h=H}^\\infty\\rho^hC_T(h),\n\\]\n\\[\nq_{m,T}=\\frac1B\\sum_{h=H}^mg_{m-h}C_T(h).\n\\]\nThe series converges absolutely. The finite universe of tail data bounds the number of possible witnesses and subsets for fixed \\(H\\), making all estimates uniform even when witness-set membership changes with phase.</p><p>Since \\(B=\\alpha m\\rho^{-m}\\) and \\(g_j=\\gamma\\rho^{-j}+O(1)\\),\n\\[\nq_{m,T}\n=\n\\frac{\\gamma}{\\alpha m}\n\\sum_{h=H}^m\\rho^hC_T(h)\n+\nO_H\\left(\\frac{m\\log(2m)}B\\right)\n=\n\\frac{\\gamma}{\\alpha m}S_T\n+o_{x\\to\\infty;H}(m^{-1}).\n\\]\nThe omitted geometric tail is \\(O_H(\\rho^m\\log(2m))\\). Thus \\(q_{m,T}=O_H(1/m)\\), and\n\\[\n(1-q_{m,T})_+^{m-H}\n=\n\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\gamma}{\\alpha_\\theta}S_T\\right)\n+o_{x\\to\\infty;H}(1).\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\n\\alpha/\\alpha_\\theta=1+O(\\log m/m),\n\\qquad\n(m-H)q_{m,T}^2=O_H(1/m).\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n\\frac{G_{m-H}}{G_m}\n=\n\\prod_{l=0}^{H-1}\\frac{(m-l)g_{m-l}}B\n=\n\\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{\\alpha_\\theta}\\right)^H\n\\rho^{H(H-1)/2}\n+o_{x\\to\\infty;H}(1). \\tag{6.12}\n\\]\nThere are boundedly many terms for fixed \\(H\\). The inclusion–exclusion expression therefore differs from (2.8) by \\(o_{x\\to\\infty;H}(1)\\), uniformly in phase. Proposition 12 proves (6.11).</p><p>Nonnegativity follows from the finite-union probability interpretation: the union probabilities and outer weights are nonnegative.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Lemma 6.6, “Convergence from a common comparison.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions.\nFix \\(f:[1,\\infty)\\to[0,1]\\). Suppose a real function \\(C_f(x)\\), independent of \\(H\\), and numbers \\(\\eta_H\\to0\\) satisfy\n\\[\n\\limsup_{x\\to\\infty}\n\\left|C_f(x)-\\frac{M_f(x;H)}{G_m}\\right|\n\\le\\eta_H. \\tag{6.13}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functions \\(A_H(f;\\cdot)\\) converge uniformly on \\([0,1)\\) to \\(A(f;\\cdot)\\), and\n\\[\nC_f(x)=A(f;\\theta(x))+o(1). \\tag{6.14}\n\\]\nIf additionally \\(c_-\\le C_f(x)\\le c_+\\) for every sufficiently large \\(x\\), then\n\\[\nc_-\\le A(f;s)\\le c_+\n\\quad(0\\le s&lt;1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 13 gives\n\\[\n\\limsup_{x\\to\\infty}|C_f(x)-A_H(f;\\theta(x))|\n\\le\\zeta_H,\n\\qquad\n\\zeta_H=\\eta_H+\\varepsilon_H\\to0.\n\\]\nEvery phase occurs at arbitrarily large real arguments. Indeed,\n\\[\n\\psi(B)=\\frac{\\log B-\\log\\log B}{\\lambda}\n\\]\nis continuous, strictly increasing for \\(B&gt;e\\), and tends to infinity. For fixed \\(s\\in[0,1)\\), solve \\(\\psi(B_n)=n+s\\) and set \\(x_n=\\exp(\\exp B_n)\\). Then \\(m(x_n)=n\\) and \\(\\theta(x_n)=s\\) exactly.</p><p>Comparing indices \\(H,K\\) along this sequence gives\n\\[\n|A_H(f;s)-A_K(f;s)|\\le\\zeta_H+\\zeta_K.\n\\]\nThe bound is independent of \\(s\\), so the sequence is uniformly Cauchy, and\n\\[\n\\sup_s|A_H(f;s)-A(f;s)|\\le\\zeta_H.\n\\]\nCombine this with the limsup estimate and send \\(H\\to\\infty\\) to obtain (6.14). If \\(C_f\\) is bounded between \\(c_-,c_+\\), apply those bounds on each exact-phase sequence and let \\(H\\to\\infty\\). No continuity in phase is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Lemma 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions.\nFix a positive integer \\(k\\), use \\(N_k,f_k\\) from Theorem 2, and \\(M_f\\) from Proposition 10. Let \\(H\\) be sufficiently large and fixed before \\(x\\to\\infty\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(\\epsilon_{H,k}\\ge0\\), tending to zero as \\(H\\to\\infty\\), such that\n\\[\n\\left|\nN_k(x)-\\frac x{\\log x}M_{f_k}(x;H)\n\\right|\n\\le\n\\{\\epsilon_{H,k}+o_{x\\to\\infty;H,k}(1)\\}\n\\frac{xG_m}{\\log x}. \\tag{7.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 9, with the stated exceptional-value and exceptional-tuple error, count tuples satisfying\n\\[\nd\\prod_{i=0}^R(p_i-1)\\le x,\\qquad\nkx&lt;p_0\\cdots p_R\\ell(d)\\le(k+1)x.\n\\]\nFix \\((d,p_1,\\ldots,p_R)\\), and put\n\\[\nD=d\\prod_{i=1}^R(p_i-1),\\qquad\nA=\\ell(d)\\prod_{i=1}^Rp_i,\n\\]\n\\[\nr=\\ell(d)/d,\\qquad\nq=\\prod_{i=1}^R(1-1/p_i),\\qquad\nT=x/D,\\qquad U=x/A=Tq/r.\n\\]\nThe admissible largest primes satisfy exactly\n\\[\np_0\\ge x^{0.9},\\qquad\nkU&lt;p_0\\le\\min\\{T+1,(k+1)U\\}. \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nThe remaining witness restrictions do not change with \\(p_0\\), since the zeroth simplex coordinate is \\(B\\).</p><p>Ignoring the lower cutoff and replacing \\(T+1\\) by \\(T\\), the interval length is\n\\[\n\\min\\{T,(k+1)U\\}-\\min\\{T,kU\\}\n=\nTf_k(r/q).\n\\]\nThe replacement changes length by at most \\(1\\). The function \\(z\\mapsto f_k(1/z)\\), extended by zero at \\(z=0\\), is \\((k+1)\\)-Lipschitz on \\([0,1]\\). Hence\n\\[\n|f_k(r/q)-f_k(r)|\n\\le\\frac{(k+1)(1-q)}r\n\\le(k+1)\\sum_{i=1}^R\\frac1{p_i}. \\tag{7.3}\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(H\\), Proposition 5 gives \\(T=x^{1-o(1)}\\) uniformly. The tail has a witness \\(w\\) bounded in terms of \\(H\\), so \\(r\\) is bounded in terms of \\(H\\); the bands bound \\(q\\) away from zero. Thus \\(U=x^{1-o(1)}\\), and \\(kU&gt;x^{0.9}\\) for large \\(x\\), making the lower cutoff inactive.</p><p>Apply the prime number theorem at both endpoints, taking the positive part if the interval is empty. Each endpoint is \\(x^{1-o(1)}\\) and \\(O_k(T)\\). Uniformly,\n\\[\n\\#\\{p_0\\text{ satisfying }(7.2)\\}\n=\n\\frac T{\\log x}\n\\left\\{\nf_k(r)\n+O_k\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^R\\frac1{p_i}\\right)\n+o_{x\\to\\infty;H,k}(1)\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThis is additive relative to \\(T/\\log x\\); no relative estimate for a short prime interval is used.</p><p>The geometrically separated bands imply\n\\[\n\\sup\\sum_{i=1}^R\\frac1{p_i}\n\\ll\\exp(-cH\\rho^{-H}),\n\\]\nuniformly in the growing number \\(R\\). Indeed \\(b_{i-1}/b_i\\ge\\rho^{-1}\\), and \\(p_i\\ge\\exp\\exp(cb_i)\\). Since \\(M_1\\ll K_HG_m\\), summation gives error at most\n\\[\n\\left\\{\nO_k(K_H\\exp(-cH\\rho^{-H}))\n+o_{x\\to\\infty;H,k}(1)\n\\right\\}\\frac{xG_m}{\\log x}.\n\\]\nTogether with Proposition 9 this proves (7.1).</p><p>The additive form is necessary: at \\(r=k\\) and \\(q&lt;1\\), \\(f_k(r)=0\\) but \\(f_k(r/q)&gt;0\\). Equation (7.3) handles this boundary.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Section 7.3, using Lemmas 7.2–7.3 and Remark 7.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Conditions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions.\nFix a positive integer \\(k\\). Use \\(N_k,f_k,M_f\\), and the scale \\(xG_m/\\log x\\) defined earlier.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If some \\(d_*\\in\\mathcal V\\) satisfies \\(\\ell(d_*)&gt;kd_*\\), there is a constant \\(c_k&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\frac{N_k(x)\\log x}{xG_m}\\ge c_k\n\\]\nfor every sufficiently large \\(x\\). This normalized count is also bounded above.</p><p>If \\(\\ell(d)\\le kd\\) for every \\(d\\in\\mathcal V\\), then\n\\[\nN_k(x)=0\\quad(x&gt;0),\n\\qquad\nA_H(f_k;s)=0\\quad(0\\le s&lt;1).\n\\]\nThe seed condition holds for \\(k=1,2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(\\ell(d_*)&gt;kd_*\\). Choose \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\n\\ell(d_*)/d_*&gt;k+2\\delta.\n\\]\nLemma 16.1 supplies \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) such that at least \\(\\eta V(x)\\) values satisfy\n\\[\n\\ell(v)/v&gt;k+2\\delta\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(x\\). Apply Lemma 16.2 with \\(\\varepsilon=\\eta/4\\). For a fixed \\(C\\), at least \\(3\\eta V(x)/4\\) values satisfy\n\\[\nk+2\\delta&lt;\\ell(v)/v\\le C. \\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nThe constants \\(\\eta,\\delta,C\\) are fixed before \\(H\\) grows.</p><p>For every sufficiently large \\(H\\), then sufficiently large \\(x\\), Proposition 9 removes at most \\(\\eta V(x)/4\\) such values. For every remaining value, its unique tuple obeys\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ell(v)}v\n=\n\\frac{\\ell(d)}d\n\\prod_{i=0}^R\\frac{p_i}{p_i-1}.\n\\]\nThe product is \\(1+O(\\epsilon_H)+o_{x\\to\\infty;H}(1)\\), uniformly in the tuple. Increase \\(H\\), then \\(x\\), so it is at most\n\\[\n\\frac{k+2\\delta}{k+\\delta}.\n\\]\nAt least \\(\\eta V(x)/2\\) distinct tuples then have tail ratio in\n\\[\nI=[k+\\delta,C].\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(M_{\\mathbf1_I}\\) be the mass with weight \\(\\mathbf1_I\\). Counting all largest primes for such data by the ordinary prime number theorem bounds their tuple count above by\n\\[\n(1+o_{x\\to\\infty;H}(1))\n\\frac x{\\log x}M_{\\mathbf1_I}(x;H).\n\\]\nComparison with \\(\\eta V(x)/2\\), then Proposition 1, gives\n\\[\nM_{\\mathbf1_I}(x;H)\\ge c_1G_m\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(H\\), then sufficiently large \\(x\\), with \\(c_1&gt;0\\) independent of \\(H\\). Since\n\\[\n\\inf_{r\\in I}f_k(r)\n\\ge\\min\\left\\{\\frac{\\delta}{k+\\delta},\\frac1C\\right\\}&gt;0,\n\\]\none obtains\n\\[\nM_{f_k}(x;H)\\ge c_2G_m \\tag{7.6}\n\\]\nwith \\(c_2&gt;0\\) independent of sufficiently large \\(H\\).</p><p>Fix such an \\(H\\) for which the error in Proposition 15 is below \\(c_2/4\\), then take \\(x\\) large enough that its remaining error is below \\(c_2/4\\). Thus\n\\[\nN_k(x)\\log x/(xG_m)\\ge c_2/2.\n\\]\nThe upper bound follows from \\(N_k(x)\\le V(x)\\) and Proposition 1.</p><p>If instead \\(\\ell(d)\\le kd\\) for every totient \\(d\\), then for \\(v\\le x\\),\n\\[\n\\ell(v)\\le kv\\le kx,\n\\]\nso \\(N_k(x)=0\\). Every weight \\(f_k(\\ell(d)/d)\\) in (2.8) is zero, hence \\(A_H(f_k;s)=0\\).</p><p>For the final assertion, original Remark 7.4 uses Ford’s example\n\\[\nd=2^{18}\\cdot257,\n\\]\na totient all of whose preimages are divisible by \\(8\\) [7, Section 7.3, p. 39]. Each preimage \\(n\\) is even and has an odd prime factor, since a power of \\(2\\) has a power of \\(2\\) as its totient. Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac n{\\varphi(n)}\n=\n\\prod_{p\\mid n}\\frac p{p-1}&gt;2,\n\\]\nand \\(\\ell(d)&gt;2d\\). This supplies the seed for \\(k=1,2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.2, “Ford’s inverse-fiber propagation.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Conditions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions.\nFix a totient \\(d\\), and write its full inverse fiber as\n\\[\n\\varphi^{-1}(d)=\\{d_1,\\ldots,d_\\kappa\\}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are constants \\(\\eta_d&gt;0\\) and \\(x_d\\) such that, for every \\(x\\ge x_d\\), at least \\(\\eta_dV(x)\\) distinct totients \\(v\\le x\\) have\n\\[\nv=d\\varphi(b),\\qquad\n\\varphi^{-1}(v)=\\{bd_1,\\ldots,bd_\\kappa\\}\n\\]\nfor some integer \\(b\\ge1\\). These values satisfy\n\\[\n\\ell(v)/v\\ge\\ell(d)/d.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the construction in the proof of Ford [7, Theorem 2, Section 5, pp. 24–26, equations (5.10)–(5.19)]. For the fixed seed \\(d\\), Ford constructs a set \\(\\mathcal B\\) and excludes at most half its members. For each remaining \\(b\\), every preimage of \\(d\\varphi(b)\\) is one of the products \\(bd_i\\). The paragraph following his equation (5.18) proves that these members give distinct totients. His concluding bound is\n\\[\n|\\mathcal B|/2\\gg_\\varepsilon d^{-1-\\varepsilon}V(x)\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(x\\). The full-fiber conclusion is explicitly recorded in [7, Section 7.3, p. 39]. Finally,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ell(v)}v\n=\n\\frac b{\\varphi(b)}\\frac{\\ell(d)}d\n\\ge\\frac{\\ell(d)}d.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.2", "orig": "Lemma 7.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Conditions.", "statement": "<p>Conditions.\nLet \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(C&lt;\\infty\\) and \\(x_0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\#\\{v\\in\\mathcal V:v\\le x,\\ \\ell(v)/v&gt;C\\}\n\\le\\varepsilon V(x)\n\\quad(x\\ge x_0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a sufficiently large fixed cut \\(H_0\\) in Proposition 5 so that its exceptional set has at most \\(\\varepsilon V(x)\\) members for all sufficiently large \\(x\\). Every remaining value has a basic witness\n\\[\nn=p_0\\cdots p_{R_0}w,\\qquad\nR_0=m-H_0,\\qquad w&lt;p_{R_0},\n\\]\nwhere \\(w\\) is bounded in terms of \\(H_0\\). The prefix primes are distinct, and their bands give a uniform bound for\n\\[\n\\prod_{i=0}^{R_0}\\frac{p_i}{p_i-1}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ell(v)}v\n\\le\\frac n{\\varphi(n)}\n=\n\\frac w{\\varphi(w)}\n\\prod_{i=0}^{R_0}\\frac{p_i}{p_i-1}\n\\le C(H_0),\n\\]\nindependently of \\(x\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 17, "cite"], [2, 0, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [2, 4, "cite"], [2, 12, "cite"], [2, 15, "cite"], [2, 16, "cite"], [2, 21, "cite"], [3, 16, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [4, 6, "cite"], [4, 8, "cite"], [4, 11, "cite"], [4, 14, "cite"], [4, 16, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [5, 14, "cite"], [6, 8, "cite"], [6, 12, "cite"], [6, 14, "cite"], [6, 20, "cite"], [6, 23, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [8, 12, "cite"], [9, 11, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [12, 20, "cite"], [12, 21, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [14, 16, "cite"], [14, 18, "cite"], [14, 20, "cite"], [15, 0, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [18, 0, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 1, "cite"], [20, 1, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 1, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [23, 21, "cite"]]});
